/* Math-lesson-style derivations. Loaded by index.html and used to override the inline derivation field of each lesson. */
const DERIVATIONS = {
  "0.7": "<span class=\"step\">Step 1: State the goal.</span>We want a formula for the variance of the sum $X+Y$ in terms of $\\\\mathrm{var}(X)$, $\\\\mathrm{var}(Y)$, and $\\\\mathrm{cov}(X,Y)$. Start from the definition of variance:$$ \\\\mathrm{var}(X+Y) = \\\\mathbb{E}\\\\big[(X+Y) - \\\\mathbb{E}(X+Y)\\\\big]^2 $$<span class=\"step\">Step 2: Push the expectation through the sum.</span>Using <span class=\"assume\">linearity of E</span>, $\\\\mathbb{E}(X+Y) = \\\\mathbb{E}X + \\\\mathbb{E}Y$. Substitute:$$ \\\\mathrm{var}(X+Y) = \\\\mathbb{E}\\\\big[(X - \\\\mathbb{E}X) + (Y - \\\\mathbb{E}Y)\\\\big]^2 $$<div class=\"note\">We have rewritten the deviation of the sum as the sum of deviations. This is the key algebraic trick.</div><span class=\"step\">Step 3: Expand the square.</span>Apply the identity $(a+b)^2 = a^2 + b^2 + 2ab$ with $a = X - \\\\mathbb{E}X$ and $b = Y - \\\\mathbb{E}Y$:$$ \\\\big[(X - \\\\mathbb{E}X) + (Y - \\\\mathbb{E}Y)\\\\big]^2 = (X - \\\\mathbb{E}X)^2 + (Y - \\\\mathbb{E}Y)^2 + 2(X - \\\\mathbb{E}X)(Y - \\\\mathbb{E}Y) $$<span class=\"step\">Step 4: Take expectations term-by-term.</span>By <span class=\"assume\">linearity of E</span>:$$ \\\\mathrm{var}(X+Y) = \\\\mathbb{E}(X - \\\\mathbb{E}X)^2 + \\\\mathbb{E}(Y - \\\\mathbb{E}Y)^2 + 2\\\\,\\\\mathbb{E}\\\\big[(X - \\\\mathbb{E}X)(Y - \\\\mathbb{E}Y)\\\\big] $$<span class=\"step\">Step 5: Recognise each term.</span>By definition:$$ \\\\mathbb{E}(X - \\\\mathbb{E}X)^2 = \\\\mathrm{var}(X) $$$$ \\\\mathbb{E}(Y - \\\\mathbb{E}Y)^2 = \\\\mathrm{var}(Y) $$$$ \\\\mathbb{E}\\\\big[(X - \\\\mathbb{E}X)(Y - \\\\mathbb{E}Y)\\\\big] = \\\\mathrm{cov}(X,Y) $$<div class=\"note\">Linearity of $\\\\mathbb{E}$ comes from linearity of integration. Bilinearity of $\\\\mathrm{cov}$ follows from expanding products inside the expectation in the same way.</div><div class=\"conclude\">$$ \\\\boxed{\\\\mathrm{var}(X+Y) = \\\\mathrm{var}(X) + \\\\mathrm{var}(Y) + 2\\\\,\\\\mathrm{cov}(X,Y)} $$</div>",

  "0.8": "<span class=\"substep\">Part A: Law of Iterated Expectations (LIE).</span><span class=\"step\">Step 1: Write the joint density as a conditional times a marginal.</span>Let $p(y,x)$ be the joint density of $(Y,X)$, $r(x)$ the marginal of $X$, and $q(y\\\\mid x)$ the conditional density of $Y$ given $X=x$. By definition:$$ p(y,x) = q(y\\\\mid x)\\\\,r(x) $$<span class=\"step\">Step 2: Compute $\\\\mathbb{E}Y$ from the joint density.</span>$$ \\\\mathbb{E}Y = \\\\int\\\\!\\\\int y\\\\,p(y,x)\\\\,dy\\\\,dx $$<span class=\"step\">Step 3: Substitute the factorisation and switch the order of integration.</span>Using <span class=\"assume\">Fubini</span> to integrate $y$ first:$$ \\\\mathbb{E}Y = \\\\int r(x)\\\\left[\\\\int y\\\\,q(y\\\\mid x)\\\\,dy\\\\right]dx $$<span class=\"step\">Step 4: Recognise the inner integral as the conditional expectation.</span>$$ \\\\int y\\\\,q(y\\\\mid x)\\\\,dy = \\\\mathbb{E}[Y\\\\mid X = x] $$Substitute back:$$ \\\\mathbb{E}Y = \\\\int r(x)\\\\,\\\\mathbb{E}[Y\\\\mid X=x]\\\\,dx = \\\\mathbb{E}\\\\big[\\\\mathbb{E}[Y\\\\mid X]\\\\big] $$<div class=\"conclude\">$$ \\\\boxed{\\\\mathbb{E}Y = \\\\mathbb{E}\\\\big[\\\\mathbb{E}[Y\\\\mid X]\\\\big]} $$</div><span class=\"substep\">Part B: Conditioning rule.</span><span class=\"step\">Step 1: Treat $g(X)$ as known once we condition on $X = x$.</span>$$ \\\\mathbb{E}\\\\big[g(X)\\\\,Y\\\\mid X=x\\\\big] = \\\\int y\\\\,g(x)\\\\,q(y\\\\mid x)\\\\,dy $$<span class=\"step\">Step 2: Pull $g(x)$ outside the integral (it does not depend on $y$).</span>$$ = g(x)\\\\int y\\\\,q(y\\\\mid x)\\\\,dy = g(x)\\\\,\\\\mathbb{E}[Y\\\\mid X=x] $$<div class=\"conclude\">$$ \\\\boxed{\\\\mathbb{E}\\\\big[g(X)Y\\\\mid X\\\\big] = g(X)\\\\,\\\\mathbb{E}[Y\\\\mid X]} $$</div><span class=\"substep\">Part C: CEF as MSE-minimiser.</span><span class=\"step\">Step 1: Set up the comparison.</span>For any candidate predictor $m(X)$, define the residual relative to the CEF and the gap to it:$$ e := Y - \\\\mathbb{E}[Y\\\\mid X], \\\\qquad g(X) := m(X) - \\\\mathbb{E}[Y\\\\mid X] $$Then $Y - m(X) = e - g(X)$.<span class=\"step\">Step 2: Expand the squared loss.</span>$$ \\\\mathbb{E}[Y - m(X)]^2 = \\\\mathbb{E}\\\\big[e - g(X)\\\\big]^2 = \\\\mathbb{E}e^2 + \\\\mathbb{E}g(X)^2 - 2\\\\,\\\\mathbb{E}[e\\\\,g(X)] $$<span class=\"step\">Step 3: Show the cross term vanishes.</span>Apply <span class=\"assume\">LIE</span> and the <span class=\"assume\">conditioning rule</span>:$$ \\\\mathbb{E}[e\\\\,g(X)] = \\\\mathbb{E}\\\\big[\\\\mathbb{E}[e\\\\,g(X)\\\\mid X]\\\\big] = \\\\mathbb{E}\\\\big[g(X)\\\\,\\\\mathbb{E}[e\\\\mid X]\\\\big] $$Now $\\\\mathbb{E}[e\\\\mid X] = \\\\mathbb{E}[Y\\\\mid X] - \\\\mathbb{E}[Y\\\\mid X] = 0$, so:$$ \\\\mathbb{E}[e\\\\,g(X)] = \\\\mathbb{E}[g(X)\\\\cdot 0] = 0 $$<span class=\"step\">Step 4: Conclude.</span>$$ \\\\mathbb{E}[Y - m(X)]^2 = \\\\mathbb{E}e^2 + \\\\mathbb{E}g(X)^2 \\\\;\\\\ge\\\\; \\\\mathbb{E}e^2 $$with equality iff $g(X) \\\\equiv 0$, i.e. iff $m(X) = \\\\mathbb{E}[Y\\\\mid X]$.<div class=\"conclude\">$$ \\\\boxed{\\\\arg\\\\min_{m(\\\\cdot)}\\\\mathbb{E}[Y - m(X)]^2 = \\\\mathbb{E}[Y\\\\mid X]} $$</div>",

  "0.15": "<span class=\"step\">Step 1: Define the confidence set by inverting the test.</span>The $1-\\\\alpha$ confidence set $\\\\mathcal{C}$ collects every candidate value $b$ that the two-sided $t$-test (at level $\\\\alpha$) would <em>not</em> reject:$$ \\\\mathcal{C} = \\\\{b : |t(b)| \\\\le c_\\\\alpha\\\\} $$where $c_\\\\alpha$ is the critical value (e.g. $1.96$ for $\\\\alpha = 0.05$).<span class=\"step\">Step 2: Substitute the $t$-statistic formula.</span>$$ t(b) = \\\\frac{\\\\hat\\\\theta - b}{\\\\mathrm{se}(\\\\hat\\\\theta)} $$So:$$ \\\\mathcal{C} = \\\\left\\\\{b : \\\\left|\\\\frac{\\\\hat\\\\theta - b}{\\\\mathrm{se}(\\\\hat\\\\theta)}\\\\right| \\\\le c_\\\\alpha\\\\right\\\\} $$<span class=\"step\">Step 3: Remove the absolute value.</span>$$ -c_\\\\alpha \\\\le \\\\frac{\\\\hat\\\\theta - b}{\\\\mathrm{se}(\\\\hat\\\\theta)} \\\\le c_\\\\alpha $$<span class=\"step\">Step 4: Multiply through by $\\\\mathrm{se}(\\\\hat\\\\theta) > 0$ (inequality direction preserved).</span>$$ -c_\\\\alpha\\\\,\\\\mathrm{se}(\\\\hat\\\\theta) \\\\le \\\\hat\\\\theta - b \\\\le c_\\\\alpha\\\\,\\\\mathrm{se}(\\\\hat\\\\theta) $$<span class=\"step\">Step 5: Solve for $b$ (subtract $\\\\hat\\\\theta$, then negate, which flips the inequalities).</span>$$ \\\\hat\\\\theta - c_\\\\alpha\\\\,\\\\mathrm{se}(\\\\hat\\\\theta) \\\\le b \\\\le \\\\hat\\\\theta + c_\\\\alpha\\\\,\\\\mathrm{se}(\\\\hat\\\\theta) $$<div class=\"conclude\">$$ \\\\boxed{\\\\mathcal{C} = \\\\big[\\\\,\\\\hat\\\\theta - c_\\\\alpha\\\\,\\\\mathrm{se}(\\\\hat\\\\theta),\\\\;\\\\hat\\\\theta + c_\\\\alpha\\\\,\\\\mathrm{se}(\\\\hat\\\\theta)\\\\,\\\\big]} $$</div><span class=\"step\">Step 6: Coverage interpretation.</span>By construction $\\\\mathbb{P}(|t(\\\\theta_0)| \\\\le c_\\\\alpha) = 1 - \\\\alpha$ when $\\\\theta_0$ is the true value. So in repeated samples $\\\\mathcal{C}$ contains the true $\\\\theta$ with probability $1 - \\\\alpha$.<div class=\"note\">The randomness lives in $\\\\mathcal{C}$, not in $\\\\theta$. Each sample produces a different interval; the long-run frequency of intervals that cover the true value is $1 - \\\\alpha$.</div>",

  "1.3": "<span class=\"step\">Step 1: State the objective.</span>The population linear regression coefficients $(\\\\rho_0, \\\\rho_1)$ minimise the mean-squared prediction error of the linear predictor $b_0 + b_1 X$ for $Y$. Write the objective:$$ C(b_0, b_1) = \\\\mathbb{E}(Y - b_0 - b_1 X)^2 $$<span class=\"step\">Step 2: First-order condition w.r.t. $b_0$.</span>Differentiate $C$ and use the chain rule on the inner $(Y - b_0 - b_1 X)$:$$ \\\\frac{\\\\partial C}{\\\\partial b_0} = -2\\\\,\\\\mathbb{E}(Y - b_0 - b_1 X) $$Set to zero and divide by $-2$:$$ \\\\mathbb{E}(Y - b_0 - b_1 X) = 0 \\\\quad (\\\\star) $$<div class=\"note\">First <strong>normal equation</strong>: residuals have mean zero in the population.</div><span class=\"step\">Step 3: First-order condition w.r.t. $b_1$.</span>$$ \\\\frac{\\\\partial C}{\\\\partial b_1} = -2\\\\,\\\\mathbb{E}\\\\big[X(Y - b_0 - b_1 X)\\\\big] $$Set to zero:$$ \\\\mathbb{E}\\\\big[X(Y - b_0 - b_1 X)\\\\big] = 0 \\\\quad (\\\\star\\\\star) $$<div class=\"note\">Second normal equation: residuals are uncorrelated with $X$ in the population.</div><span class=\"step\">Step 4: Solve $(\\\\star)$ for the intercept.</span>Distribute using <span class=\"assume\">linearity of E</span>:$$ \\\\mathbb{E}Y - b_0 - b_1\\\\,\\\\mathbb{E}X = 0 $$$$ \\\\rho_0 = \\\\mathbb{E}Y - \\\\rho_1\\\\,\\\\mathbb{E}X $$<span class=\"step\">Step 5: Substitute into $(\\\\star\\\\star)$.</span>Replace $b_0$ inside the brackets with $\\\\mathbb{E}Y - \\\\rho_1\\\\,\\\\mathbb{E}X$:$$ \\\\mathbb{E}\\\\big[X\\\\big((Y - \\\\mathbb{E}Y) - \\\\rho_1(X - \\\\mathbb{E}X)\\\\big)\\\\big] = 0 $$Distribute:$$ \\\\mathbb{E}[X(Y - \\\\mathbb{E}Y)] - \\\\rho_1\\\\,\\\\mathbb{E}[X(X - \\\\mathbb{E}X)] = 0 $$<span class=\"step\">Step 6: Recognise covariance and variance.</span>$$ \\\\mathbb{E}[X(Y - \\\\mathbb{E}Y)] = \\\\mathrm{cov}(X,Y) $$$$ \\\\mathbb{E}[X(X - \\\\mathbb{E}X)] = \\\\mathrm{var}(X) $$<div class=\"note\">$\\\\mathbb{E}[X(Y - \\\\mathbb{E}Y)] = \\\\mathbb{E}XY - \\\\mathbb{E}X\\\\,\\\\mathbb{E}Y = \\\\mathrm{cov}(X,Y)$. Similarly for variance.</div>$$ \\\\mathrm{cov}(X,Y) - \\\\rho_1\\\\,\\\\mathrm{var}(X) = 0 $$<span class=\"step\">Step 7: Solve for the slope.</span>$$ \\\\rho_1 = \\\\frac{\\\\mathrm{cov}(X,Y)}{\\\\mathrm{var}(X)} $$<div class=\"conclude\">$$ \\\\boxed{\\\\rho_1 = \\\\dfrac{\\\\mathrm{cov}(X,Y)}{\\\\mathrm{var}(X)}, \\\\qquad \\\\rho_0 = \\\\mathbb{E}Y - \\\\rho_1\\\\,\\\\mathbb{E}X} $$</div><div class=\"note\"><strong>Uniqueness.</strong> Any other $(\\\\gamma_0, \\\\gamma_1, v)$ satisfying $Y = \\\\gamma_0 + \\\\gamma_1 X + v$ with $\\\\mathbb{E}v = \\\\mathbb{E}Xv = 0$ would solve the same two normal equations, which have a unique solution. So $(\\\\gamma_0, \\\\gamma_1) = (\\\\rho_0, \\\\rho_1)$.</div>",

  "1.4": "<span class=\"substep\">Part A: Causal $=$ regression under OR.</span><span class=\"step\">Step 1: State the causal model.</span>Assume$$ Y = \\\\beta_0 + \\\\beta_1 X + u $$with <span class=\"assume\">OR: $\\\\mathbb{E}u = 0$ and $\\\\mathbb{E}Xu = 0$</span>.<span class=\"step\">Step 2: Substitute into the population normal equations from 1.3.</span>The normal equations characterising $(\\\\rho_0, \\\\rho_1)$ are:$$ \\\\mathbb{E}(Y - \\\\rho_0 - \\\\rho_1 X) = 0 $$$$ \\\\mathbb{E}\\\\big[X(Y - \\\\rho_0 - \\\\rho_1 X)\\\\big] = 0 $$Plug in $Y - \\\\beta_0 - \\\\beta_1 X = u$:$$ \\\\mathbb{E}(Y - \\\\beta_0 - \\\\beta_1 X) = \\\\mathbb{E}u = 0 \\\\;\\\\checkmark $$$$ \\\\mathbb{E}\\\\big[X(Y - \\\\beta_0 - \\\\beta_1 X)\\\\big] = \\\\mathbb{E}Xu = 0 \\\\;\\\\checkmark $$Both hold by OR.<span class=\"step\">Step 3: Apply uniqueness.</span>Since the normal equations have a unique solution and $(\\\\beta_0, \\\\beta_1)$ satisfies them, we must have $(\\\\beta_0, \\\\beta_1) = (\\\\rho_0, \\\\rho_1)$.<div class=\"conclude\">$$ \\\\boxed{\\\\text{Under OR, the causal slope equals the population regression slope.}} $$</div><span class=\"substep\">Part B: MI implies OR.</span><span class=\"step\">Step 1: Apply LIE to $\\\\mathbb{E}u$.</span>By <span class=\"assume\">LIE</span>:$$ \\\\mathbb{E}u = \\\\mathbb{E}\\\\big[\\\\mathbb{E}[u\\\\mid X]\\\\big] $$Under <span class=\"assume\">MI: $\\\\mathbb{E}[u\\\\mid X] = 0$</span>:$$ \\\\mathbb{E}u = \\\\mathbb{E}[0] = 0 $$<span class=\"step\">Step 2: Apply LIE and the conditioning rule to $\\\\mathbb{E}Xu$.</span>$$ \\\\mathbb{E}Xu = \\\\mathbb{E}\\\\big[\\\\mathbb{E}[Xu\\\\mid X]\\\\big] $$By the <span class=\"assume\">conditioning rule</span>, $\\\\mathbb{E}[Xu\\\\mid X] = X\\\\,\\\\mathbb{E}[u\\\\mid X] = X\\\\cdot 0 = 0$. Therefore:$$ \\\\mathbb{E}Xu = \\\\mathbb{E}[0] = 0 $$<div class=\"conclude\">$$ \\\\boxed{\\\\text{MI} \\\\;\\\\Rightarrow\\\\; \\\\text{OR}} $$</div>",

  "1.5": "<span class=\"substep\">Part A: MI implies the CEF is linear.</span><span class=\"step\">Step 1: Take conditional expectation of the structural equation.</span>From $Y = \\\\beta_0 + \\\\beta_1 X + u$, condition on $X$:$$ \\\\mathbb{E}[Y\\\\mid X] = \\\\mathbb{E}[\\\\beta_0\\\\mid X] + \\\\mathbb{E}[\\\\beta_1 X\\\\mid X] + \\\\mathbb{E}[u\\\\mid X] $$<span class=\"step\">Step 2: Simplify.</span>The constants come out and $X$ is known given $X$:$$ \\\\mathbb{E}[Y\\\\mid X] = \\\\beta_0 + \\\\beta_1 X + \\\\mathbb{E}[u\\\\mid X] $$<span class=\"step\">Step 3: Apply MI.</span>Under <span class=\"assume\">MI: $\\\\mathbb{E}[u\\\\mid X] = 0$</span>:$$ \\\\mathbb{E}[Y\\\\mid X] = \\\\beta_0 + \\\\beta_1 X $$<div class=\"conclude\">$$ \\\\boxed{\\\\text{MI} \\\\;\\\\Rightarrow\\\\; \\\\mathbb{E}[Y\\\\mid X] \\\\text{ is linear in } X} $$</div><span class=\"substep\">Part B: CEF residual satisfies the regression normal equations.</span><span class=\"step\">Step 1: Define the CEF residual.</span>$$ e := Y - \\\\mathbb{E}[Y\\\\mid X] $$By construction $\\\\mathbb{E}[e\\\\mid X] = 0$.<span class=\"step\">Step 2: Show $\\\\mathbb{E}e = 0$.</span>By <span class=\"assume\">LIE</span>:$$ \\\\mathbb{E}e = \\\\mathbb{E}\\\\big[\\\\mathbb{E}[e\\\\mid X]\\\\big] = \\\\mathbb{E}[0] = 0 $$<span class=\"step\">Step 3: Show $\\\\mathbb{E}Xe = 0$.</span>By <span class=\"assume\">LIE</span> and the <span class=\"assume\">conditioning rule</span>:$$ \\\\mathbb{E}Xe = \\\\mathbb{E}\\\\big[\\\\mathbb{E}[Xe\\\\mid X]\\\\big] = \\\\mathbb{E}\\\\big[X\\\\,\\\\mathbb{E}[e\\\\mid X]\\\\big] = \\\\mathbb{E}[X\\\\cdot 0] = 0 $$<div class=\"note\">$e$ satisfies the same normal equations as the population regression residual. Combined with Part A, this means the CEF and the population linear regression coincide whenever the CEF is linear.</div><div class=\"conclude\">$$ \\\\boxed{\\\\mathbb{E}e = 0,\\\\quad \\\\mathbb{E}Xe = 0} $$</div>",

  "1.6": "<span class=\"step\">Step 1: Sample analogue of the population objective.</span>Replace $\\\\mathbb{E}$ with the sample average $\\\\hat{\\\\mathbb{E}}$. The OLS estimator minimises:$$ \\\\hat{\\\\mathbb{E}}(Y - b_0 - b_1 X)^2 = \\\\frac{1}{n}\\\\sum_{i=1}^{n}(Y_i - b_0 - b_1 X_i)^2 $$<span class=\"step\">Step 2: First-order condition w.r.t. $b_0$.</span>Differentiate and set to zero:$$ -\\\\frac{2}{n}\\\\sum_{i=1}^{n}(Y_i - \\\\hat\\\\beta_0 - \\\\hat\\\\beta_1 X_i) = 0 $$Define the in-sample residual $\\\\hat u_i := Y_i - \\\\hat\\\\beta_0 - \\\\hat\\\\beta_1 X_i$. Then:$$ \\\\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\\\hat u_i = 0 \\\\quad (\\\\star) $$<div class=\"note\">First sample normal equation: in-sample residuals sum to zero.</div><span class=\"step\">Step 3: First-order condition w.r.t. $b_1$.</span>$$ -\\\\frac{2}{n}\\\\sum_{i=1}^{n}X_i(Y_i - \\\\hat\\\\beta_0 - \\\\hat\\\\beta_1 X_i) = 0 $$$$ \\\\sum_{i=1}^{n}X_i\\\\,\\\\hat u_i = 0 \\\\quad (\\\\star\\\\star) $$<div class=\"note\">Second sample normal equation: in-sample residuals are orthogonal to the regressor.</div><span class=\"step\">Step 4: Solve $(\\\\star)$ and $(\\\\star\\\\star)$ as in 1.3.</span>The two equations are the sample analogues of the population normal equations, with $\\\\mathbb{E}$ replaced by $\\\\hat{\\\\mathbb{E}}$. The same algebra yields the closed-form OLS estimators:$$ \\\\hat\\\\beta_1 = \\\\frac{\\\\hat{\\\\mathrm{cov}}(X,Y)}{\\\\hat{\\\\mathrm{var}}(X)},\\\\qquad \\\\hat\\\\beta_0 = \\\\overline{Y} - \\\\hat\\\\beta_1\\\\,\\\\overline{X} $$<div class=\"conclude\">$$ \\\\boxed{\\\\hat\\\\beta_1 = \\\\dfrac{\\\\hat{\\\\mathrm{cov}}(X,Y)}{\\\\hat{\\\\mathrm{var}}(X)},\\\\qquad \\\\hat\\\\beta_0 = \\\\overline{Y} - \\\\hat\\\\beta_1\\\\,\\\\overline{X}} $$</div>",

  "1.7": "<span class=\"substep\">Part A: Consistency of $\\\\hat\\\\beta_1$.</span><span class=\"step\">Step 1: Write the OLS slope in covariance form.</span>From 1.6:$$ \\\\hat\\\\beta_1 = \\\\frac{\\\\hat{\\\\mathrm{cov}}(X,Y)}{\\\\hat{\\\\mathrm{var}}(X)} $$<span class=\"step\">Step 2: Apply the LLN to numerator and denominator.</span>By the <span class=\"assume\">LLN</span> (using <span class=\"assume\">iid sampling</span> and finite second moments):$$ \\\\hat{\\\\mathrm{cov}}(X,Y) \\\\xrightarrow{p} \\\\mathrm{cov}(X,Y) $$$$ \\\\hat{\\\\mathrm{var}}(X) \\\\xrightarrow{p} \\\\mathrm{var}(X) $$<span class=\"step\">Step 3: Apply the Continuous Mapping Theorem.</span>Since division by a nonzero limit is continuous:$$ \\\\hat\\\\beta_1 \\\\xrightarrow{p} \\\\frac{\\\\mathrm{cov}(X,Y)}{\\\\mathrm{var}(X)} = \\\\rho_1 $$<span class=\"step\">Step 4: Use OR to identify $\\\\rho_1$ with $\\\\beta_1$.</span>Under <span class=\"assume\">OR</span>, by 1.4, $\\\\rho_1 = \\\\beta_1$. Hence $\\\\hat\\\\beta_1 \\\\xrightarrow{p} \\\\beta_1$.<span class=\"substep\">Part B: Asymptotic normality.</span><span class=\"step\">Step 1: Substitute the structural equation.</span>Using $Y_i = \\\\beta_0 + \\\\beta_1 X_i + u_i$:$$ \\\\hat{\\\\mathrm{cov}}(X,Y) = \\\\hat{\\\\mathrm{cov}}(X,\\\\beta_0 + \\\\beta_1 X + u) = \\\\beta_1\\\\,\\\\hat{\\\\mathrm{var}}(X) + \\\\hat{\\\\mathrm{cov}}(X,u) $$So:$$ \\\\hat\\\\beta_1 - \\\\beta_1 = \\\\frac{\\\\hat{\\\\mathrm{cov}}(X,u)}{\\\\hat{\\\\mathrm{var}}(X)} $$<span class=\"step\">Step 2: Multiply by $\\\\sqrt n$ and rewrite.</span>$$ \\\\sqrt n(\\\\hat\\\\beta_1 - \\\\beta_1) = \\\\frac{n^{-1/2}\\\\sum_{i=1}^n (X_i - \\\\overline X)u_i}{n^{-1}\\\\sum_{i=1}^n (X_i - \\\\overline X)^2} $$<span class=\"step\">Step 3: Limit of the denominator.</span>By the <span class=\"assume\">LLN</span>:$$ n^{-1}\\\\sum_{i=1}^n (X_i - \\\\overline X)^2 \\\\xrightarrow{p} \\\\mathrm{var}(X) =: \\\\sigma_X^2 $$<span class=\"step\">Step 4: Limit of the numerator via CLT.</span>The summands $(X_i - \\\\mu_X)u_i$ are <span class=\"assume\">iid</span> with mean$$ \\\\mathbb{E}[(X - \\\\mu_X)u] = \\\\mathbb{E}Xu - \\\\mu_X\\\\mathbb{E}u = 0 - 0 = 0 $$by <span class=\"assume\">OR</span>, and variance $\\\\mathbb{E}[(X - \\\\mu_X)^2 u^2]$ (finite under <span class=\"assume\">finite fourth moments</span>). By the <span class=\"assume\">CLT</span>:$$ n^{-1/2}\\\\sum_{i=1}^n (X_i - \\\\mu_X)u_i \\\\xrightarrow{d} N\\\\!\\\\big(0,\\\\;\\\\mathbb{E}(X - \\\\mu_X)^2 u^2\\\\big) $$The numerator above uses $\\\\overline X$ rather than $\\\\mu_X$, but $\\\\overline X \\\\xrightarrow{p} \\\\mu_X$ so the difference is asymptotically negligible by <span class=\"assume\">Slutsky</span>.<span class=\"step\">Step 5: Combine via Slutsky.</span>Ratio of $\\\\xrightarrow{d} N$ over $\\\\xrightarrow{p}$ constant:$$ \\\\sqrt n(\\\\hat\\\\beta_1 - \\\\beta_1) \\\\xrightarrow{d} N\\\\!\\\\left(0,\\\\;\\\\frac{\\\\mathbb{E}(X - \\\\mu_X)^2 u^2}{[\\\\mathrm{var}(X)]^2}\\\\right) $$<div class=\"conclude\">$$ \\\\boxed{\\\\sqrt n(\\\\hat\\\\beta_1 - \\\\beta_1) \\\\xrightarrow{d} N(0,\\\\,\\\\omega_{\\\\beta_1}^2),\\\\quad \\\\omega_{\\\\beta_1}^2 = \\\\frac{\\\\mathbb{E}(X - \\\\mu_X)^2 u^2}{[\\\\mathrm{var}(X)]^2}} $$</div>",

  "1.9": "<span class=\"step\">Step 1: Define the auxiliary regression.</span>Regress $X_1$ on $X_2$ in the population:$$ X_1 = \\\\pi_0 + \\\\pi_2 X_2 + \\\\tilde X_1 $$By the normal equations for this regression:$$ \\\\mathbb{E}\\\\tilde X_1 = 0,\\\\qquad \\\\mathbb{E}[X_2\\\\,\\\\tilde X_1] = 0 $$<div class=\"note\">$\\\\tilde X_1$ is the part of $X_1$ that is linearly unrelated to $X_2$.</div><span class=\"step\">Step 2: Substitute into the structural equation.</span>$$ Y = \\\\beta_0 + \\\\beta_1 X_1 + \\\\beta_2 X_2 + u $$Replace $X_1 = \\\\pi_0 + \\\\pi_2 X_2 + \\\\tilde X_1$:$$ Y = \\\\beta_0 + \\\\beta_1(\\\\pi_0 + \\\\pi_2 X_2 + \\\\tilde X_1) + \\\\beta_2 X_2 + u $$Group terms:$$ Y = (\\\\beta_0 + \\\\beta_1 \\\\pi_0) + \\\\beta_1\\\\,\\\\tilde X_1 + (\\\\beta_2 + \\\\beta_1 \\\\pi_2)X_2 + u $$<span class=\"step\">Step 3: Take covariance with $\\\\tilde X_1$.</span>Use bilinearity of $\\\\mathrm{cov}$:$$ \\\\mathrm{cov}(Y, \\\\tilde X_1) = \\\\beta_1\\\\,\\\\mathrm{var}(\\\\tilde X_1) + (\\\\beta_2 + \\\\beta_1\\\\pi_2)\\\\,\\\\mathrm{cov}(X_2, \\\\tilde X_1) + \\\\mathrm{cov}(u, \\\\tilde X_1) $$<span class=\"step\">Step 4: Kill the off-diagonal terms.</span>By construction $\\\\mathrm{cov}(X_2, \\\\tilde X_1) = 0$. Under <span class=\"assume\">OR for the structural equation</span>, $u$ is uncorrelated with $X_1$ and $X_2$, hence with $\\\\tilde X_1 = X_1 - \\\\pi_0 - \\\\pi_2 X_2$. So $\\\\mathrm{cov}(u, \\\\tilde X_1) = 0$.$$ \\\\mathrm{cov}(Y, \\\\tilde X_1) = \\\\beta_1\\\\,\\\\mathrm{var}(\\\\tilde X_1) $$<span class=\"step\">Step 5: Solve for $\\\\beta_1$.</span><div class=\"conclude\">$$ \\\\boxed{\\\\beta_1 = \\\\dfrac{\\\\mathrm{cov}(Y, \\\\tilde X_1)}{\\\\mathrm{var}(\\\\tilde X_1)}} $$</div><div class=\"note\">This is <strong>Frisch-Waugh-Lovell</strong>: the multiple-regression slope on $X_1$ equals the simple-regression slope of $Y$ on the part of $X_1$ orthogonal to $X_2$.</div>",

  "1.10": "<span class=\"step\">Step 1: Write the short-regression slope.</span>Regress $Y$ on $X_1$ alone. By 1.3:$$ \\\\gamma_1 = \\\\frac{\\\\mathrm{cov}(Y, X_1)}{\\\\mathrm{var}(X_1)} $$<span class=\"step\">Step 2: Substitute the long (true) model.</span>Assume the true DGP is $Y = \\\\beta_0 + \\\\beta_1 X_1 + \\\\beta_2 X_2 + u$ with <span class=\"assume\">OR: $\\\\mathrm{cov}(u, X_1) = \\\\mathrm{cov}(u, X_2) = 0$</span>. Then by bilinearity of $\\\\mathrm{cov}$:$$ \\\\mathrm{cov}(Y, X_1) = \\\\mathrm{cov}(\\\\beta_0 + \\\\beta_1 X_1 + \\\\beta_2 X_2 + u,\\\\,X_1) $$$$ = \\\\beta_1\\\\,\\\\mathrm{var}(X_1) + \\\\beta_2\\\\,\\\\mathrm{cov}(X_1, X_2) + \\\\mathrm{cov}(u, X_1) $$<span class=\"step\">Step 3: Drop the OR-zero term.</span>$\\\\mathrm{cov}(u, X_1) = 0$, so:$$ \\\\mathrm{cov}(Y, X_1) = \\\\beta_1\\\\,\\\\mathrm{var}(X_1) + \\\\beta_2\\\\,\\\\mathrm{cov}(X_1, X_2) $$<span class=\"step\">Step 4: Divide by $\\\\mathrm{var}(X_1)$.</span>$$ \\\\gamma_1 = \\\\beta_1 + \\\\beta_2\\\\,\\\\frac{\\\\mathrm{cov}(X_1, X_2)}{\\\\mathrm{var}(X_1)} $$<span class=\"step\">Step 5: Recognise the auxiliary slope.</span>$\\\\pi_1 := \\\\mathrm{cov}(X_1, X_2)/\\\\mathrm{var}(X_1)$ is the slope from regressing $X_2$ on $X_1$. So:<div class=\"conclude\">$$ \\\\boxed{\\\\gamma_1 = \\\\beta_1 + \\\\beta_2\\\\,\\\\pi_1} $$</div><div class=\"note\"><strong>Omitted-variable bias direction.</strong> $\\\\mathrm{sign}(\\\\gamma_1 - \\\\beta_1) = \\\\mathrm{sign}(\\\\beta_2)\\\\cdot\\\\mathrm{sign}(\\\\pi_1)$. Bias is zero iff $\\\\beta_2 = 0$ (omitted variable doesn't matter) or $\\\\pi_1 = 0$ (omitted variable is uncorrelated with the included regressor).</div>",

  "1.11": "<span class=\"step\">Step 1: State the proxy and structural models.</span>True (long) model:$$ Y = \\\\beta_0 + \\\\beta_1 X_1 + \\\\beta_2 X_2 + u $$Proxy generating equation for the unobserved $X_2$:$$ X_2 = \\\\pi_0 + \\\\pi_1 X_1 + \\\\pi_2 W_2 + v $$where $W_2$ is the observed proxy.<span class=\"step\">Step 2: Substitute the proxy equation into the structural model.</span>$$ Y = \\\\beta_0 + \\\\beta_1 X_1 + \\\\beta_2(\\\\pi_0 + \\\\pi_1 X_1 + \\\\pi_2 W_2 + v) + u $$Collect terms:$$ Y = (\\\\beta_0 + \\\\beta_2\\\\pi_0) + (\\\\beta_1 + \\\\beta_2\\\\pi_1)X_1 + \\\\beta_2\\\\pi_2 W_2 + (u + \\\\beta_2 v) $$Define the estimable model:$$ Y = \\\\delta_0 + \\\\delta_1 X_1 + \\\\delta_2 W_2 + \\\\eta $$with$$ \\\\delta_1 = \\\\beta_1 + \\\\beta_2\\\\pi_1,\\\\quad \\\\delta_2 = \\\\beta_2\\\\pi_2,\\\\quad \\\\eta = u + \\\\beta_2 v $$<span class=\"step\">Step 3: List conditions for OLS to recover $\\\\beta_1$.</span>We need $\\\\delta_1 = \\\\beta_1$ and OR for the new error $\\\\eta$.<span class=\"substep\">Condition (i): $\\\\pi_2 \\\\ne 0$ (proxy is informative about $X_2$).</span>Otherwise $\\\\delta_2 = 0$ and $W_2$ contributes nothing.<span class=\"substep\">Condition (ii): <span class=\"assume\">$\\\\mathrm{cov}(W_2, u) = 0$ and $\\\\mathrm{cov}(W_2, v) = 0$</span>.</span>Then $\\\\mathrm{cov}(W_2, \\\\eta) = \\\\mathrm{cov}(W_2, u) + \\\\beta_2\\\\,\\\\mathrm{cov}(W_2, v) = 0$.<span class=\"substep\">Condition (iii): $\\\\pi_1 = 0$ (proxy soaks up all the $X_1$-relevant variation in $X_2$).</span>Then $\\\\delta_1 = \\\\beta_1 + \\\\beta_2\\\\cdot 0 = \\\\beta_1$.<span class=\"step\">Step 4: Conclude.</span>Under (i)-(iii) and the original <span class=\"assume\">OR on $u$</span>, $\\\\eta$ satisfies OR with respect to $(X_1, W_2)$, so OLS in the proxy model is consistent for $\\\\beta_1$.<div class=\"conclude\">$$ \\\\boxed{\\\\hat\\\\delta_1 \\\\xrightarrow{p} \\\\beta_1 \\\\;\\\\text{ when } \\\\pi_2 \\\\ne 0,\\\\;\\\\pi_1 = 0,\\\\;\\\\mathrm{cov}(W_2,\\\\eta)=0} $$</div>",

  "2.1": "<div class=\"note\">See lesson 1.7 for the full derivation: $\\\\hat\\\\beta_1 \\\\xrightarrow{p} \\\\beta_1$ via LLN $+$ Continuous Mapping (consistency); $\\\\sqrt n(\\\\hat\\\\beta_1 - \\\\beta_1) \\\\xrightarrow{d} N(0, \\\\omega_{\\\\beta_1}^2)$ via CLT $+$ Slutsky (asymptotic normality).</div><div class=\"conclude\">$$ \\\\boxed{\\\\sqrt n(\\\\hat\\\\beta_1 - \\\\beta_1) \\\\xrightarrow{d} N\\\\!\\\\left(0,\\\\;\\\\frac{\\\\mathbb{E}(X - \\\\mu_X)^2 u^2}{[\\\\mathrm{var}(X)]^2}\\\\right)} $$</div>",

  "2.2": "<span class=\"step\">Step 1: Recall the heteroskedastic asymptotic variance.</span>From 1.7:$$ \\\\omega_{\\\\beta_1}^2 = \\\\frac{\\\\mathbb{E}(X - \\\\mu_X)^2 u^2}{[\\\\mathrm{var}(X)]^2} $$<span class=\"step\">Step 2: Invoke homoskedasticity.</span><span class=\"assume\">Homoskedasticity: $\\\\mathbb{E}[u^2\\\\mid X] = \\\\sigma_u^2$</span> (constant).<span class=\"step\">Step 3: Simplify the numerator using LIE.</span>By <span class=\"assume\">LIE</span> and the <span class=\"assume\">conditioning rule</span>:$$ \\\\mathbb{E}(X - \\\\mu_X)^2 u^2 = \\\\mathbb{E}\\\\big[(X - \\\\mu_X)^2\\\\,\\\\mathbb{E}[u^2\\\\mid X]\\\\big] $$Plug in the constant conditional variance:$$ = \\\\mathbb{E}\\\\big[(X - \\\\mu_X)^2 \\\\sigma_u^2\\\\big] = \\\\sigma_u^2\\\\,\\\\mathbb{E}(X - \\\\mu_X)^2 = \\\\sigma_u^2\\\\,\\\\mathrm{var}(X) $$<span class=\"step\">Step 4: Substitute back.</span>$$ \\\\omega_{\\\\beta_1}^2 = \\\\frac{\\\\sigma_u^2\\\\,\\\\mathrm{var}(X)}{[\\\\mathrm{var}(X)]^2} = \\\\frac{\\\\sigma_u^2}{\\\\mathrm{var}(X)} $$<div class=\"conclude\">$$ \\\\boxed{\\\\omega_{\\\\beta_1}^2 = \\\\dfrac{\\\\sigma_u^2}{\\\\mathrm{var}(X)}} $$</div><span class=\"step\">Step 5: Multivariate generalisation via FWL.</span>By <span class=\"assume\">FWL</span>, $\\\\hat\\\\beta_l = \\\\hat{\\\\mathrm{cov}}(Y, \\\\tilde X_l)/\\\\hat{\\\\mathrm{var}}(\\\\tilde X_l)$ where $\\\\tilde X_l$ is the residual from regressing $X_l$ on the other regressors. The single-regressor formula carries over with $X$ replaced by $\\\\tilde X_l$:$$ \\\\omega_{\\\\beta_l}^2 = \\\\frac{\\\\sigma_u^2}{\\\\mathrm{var}(\\\\tilde X_l)} $$",

  "2.4": "<span class=\"substep\">Part A: Two-sided test.</span><span class=\"step\">Step 1: Write the $t$-statistic under $H_0$.</span>Test $H_0: \\\\beta_l = c$ vs.\\\\ $H_1: \\\\beta_l \\\\ne c$. Under $H_0$, the true value is $c$, so:$$ t = \\\\frac{\\\\sqrt n(\\\\hat\\\\beta_l - c)}{\\\\hat\\\\omega_{\\\\beta_l}} = \\\\frac{\\\\sqrt n(\\\\hat\\\\beta_l - \\\\beta_l)}{\\\\hat\\\\omega_{\\\\beta_l}} $$<span class=\"step\">Step 2: Limit of the numerator.</span>By 1.7 (LLN $+$ CLT $+$ Slutsky):$$ \\\\sqrt n(\\\\hat\\\\beta_l - \\\\beta_l) \\\\xrightarrow{d} N(0,\\\\,\\\\omega_{\\\\beta_l}^2) $$<span class=\"step\">Step 3: Limit of the denominator.</span>By <span class=\"assume\">SE consistency</span>:$$ \\\\hat\\\\omega_{\\\\beta_l} \\\\xrightarrow{p} \\\\omega_{\\\\beta_l} $$<span class=\"step\">Step 4: Combine via Slutsky.</span>$$ t = \\\\frac{\\\\sqrt n(\\\\hat\\\\beta_l - \\\\beta_l)}{\\\\hat\\\\omega_{\\\\beta_l}} \\\\xrightarrow{d} \\\\frac{N(0,\\\\,\\\\omega_{\\\\beta_l}^2)}{\\\\omega_{\\\\beta_l}} = N(0,1) $$<div class=\"conclude\">$$ \\\\boxed{t \\\\xrightarrow{d} N(0,1) \\\\text{ under } H_0} $$</div><span class=\"substep\">Part B: One-sided test.</span><span class=\"step\">Step 1: Decompose the $t$-statistic under $H_1: \\\\beta_l > c$.</span>$$ t = \\\\frac{\\\\sqrt n(\\\\hat\\\\beta_l - c)}{\\\\hat\\\\omega_{\\\\beta_l}} = \\\\underbrace{\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt n(\\\\hat\\\\beta_l - \\\\beta_l)}{\\\\hat\\\\omega_{\\\\beta_l}}}_{\\\\xrightarrow{d} N(0,1)} + \\\\underbrace{\\\\frac{\\\\sqrt n(\\\\beta_l - c)}{\\\\hat\\\\omega_{\\\\beta_l}}}_{\\\\to +\\\\infty} $$<span class=\"step\">Step 2: Conclude.</span>Under $H_1$, $\\\\beta_l - c > 0$ so the deterministic term diverges to $+\\\\infty$, giving asymptotic power $1$ against the alternative.",

  "2.5": "<div class=\"note\">See lesson 0.15 for the full inversion: $\\\\mathcal{C} = \\\\{b : |t(b)| \\\\le c_\\\\alpha\\\\}$ rearranges to the symmetric interval $[\\\\hat\\\\beta_l - c_\\\\alpha\\\\hat\\\\omega/\\\\sqrt n,\\\\;\\\\hat\\\\beta_l + c_\\\\alpha\\\\hat\\\\omega/\\\\sqrt n]$ around the point estimate.</div><div class=\"conclude\">$$ \\\\boxed{\\\\mathcal{C}_{1-\\\\alpha} = \\\\big[\\\\hat\\\\beta_l - c_\\\\alpha\\\\,\\\\mathrm{se}(\\\\hat\\\\beta_l),\\\\;\\\\hat\\\\beta_l + c_\\\\alpha\\\\,\\\\mathrm{se}(\\\\hat\\\\beta_l)\\\\big]} $$</div>",

  "2.6": "<span class=\"step\">Step 1: Recall the null distribution.</span>By 2.4, under $H_0$ the test statistic satisfies $T \\\\xrightarrow{d} N(0,1)$, so the limiting CDF is the standard normal $\\\\Phi$.<span class=\"step\">Step 2: Define the p-value.</span>The two-sided p-value is the probability of observing a $t$ at least as extreme as $t_{\\\\mathrm{obs}}$:$$ p = \\\\mathbb{P}\\\\big(|T| \\\\ge |t_{\\\\mathrm{obs}}|\\\\,\\\\big|\\\\,H_0\\\\big) = 2\\\\big(1 - \\\\Phi(|t_{\\\\mathrm{obs}}|)\\\\big) $$<span class=\"step\">Step 3: Show p-value rejection equivalence.</span>The two-sided test rejects iff $|t_{\\\\mathrm{obs}}| > c_\\\\alpha$. Since $\\\\Phi$ is monotonic, $|t_{\\\\mathrm{obs}}| > c_\\\\alpha \\\\Leftrightarrow 2(1 - \\\\Phi(|t_{\\\\mathrm{obs}}|)) < \\\\alpha$, i.e. $p < \\\\alpha$.<div class=\"conclude\">$$ \\\\boxed{\\\\text{Reject } H_0 \\\\text{ at level } \\\\alpha \\\\iff p < \\\\alpha} $$</div><div class=\"note\">The p-value is the smallest level at which $H_0$ would be rejected by the observed sample.</div>",

  "2.7": "<span class=\"step\">Step 1: Why $SSR_r \\\\ge SSR_u$.</span>The unrestricted model nests the restricted one, so OLS in the unrestricted model achieves at least as small a sum of squared residuals. Imposing $q$ linear restrictions cannot decrease $SSR$:$$ SSR_r \\\\ge SSR_u \\\\;\\\\Rightarrow\\\\; SSR_r - SSR_u \\\\ge 0 $$<span class=\"step\">Step 2: Distribution of the gap under $H_0$.</span>Under <span class=\"assume\">$H_0$ (the $q$ restrictions hold)</span>, with <span class=\"assume\">iid sampling</span> and <span class=\"assume\">finite fourth moments</span>:$$ \\\\frac{SSR_r - SSR_u}{\\\\sigma_u^2} \\\\xrightarrow{d} \\\\chi^2_q $$<span class=\"step\">Step 3: Consistency of the variance estimator.</span>$$ \\\\frac{SSR_u}{n - k - 1} \\\\xrightarrow{p} \\\\sigma_u^2 $$<span class=\"step\">Step 4: Form the pivotal F.</span>Divide the chi-square by $q$ and ratio against the variance estimator:$$ F = \\\\frac{(SSR_r - SSR_u)/q}{SSR_u/(n - k - 1)} \\\\xrightarrow{d} \\\\frac{\\\\chi^2_q/q}{1} = F_{q,\\\\infty} $$<span class=\"step\">Step 5: $R^2$ form.</span>Using $SSR = TSS(1 - R^2)$:$$ SSR_r - SSR_u = TSS(R^2_u - R^2_r) $$$$ F = \\\\frac{(R^2_u - R^2_r)/q}{(1 - R^2_u)/(n - k - 1)} $$<div class=\"conclude\">$$ \\\\boxed{F = \\\\dfrac{(SSR_r - SSR_u)/q}{SSR_u/(n - k - 1)} \\\\xrightarrow{d} F_{q,\\\\infty} \\\\text{ under } H_0} $$</div>",

  "2.11": "<span class=\"step\">Step 1: Compute the marginal effect from the quadratic model.</span>Take the partial derivative of $Y$ with respect to $X$ in $Y = \\\\beta_0 + \\\\beta_1 X + \\\\beta_2 X^2 + u$:$$ \\\\frac{\\\\partial Y}{\\\\partial X} = \\\\beta_1 + 2\\\\beta_2 X $$<span class=\"step\">Step 2: Estimate the marginal effect at a chosen point $x$.</span>$$ \\\\widehat{\\\\partial Y/\\\\partial X}\\\\big|_{X = x} = \\\\hat\\\\beta_1 + 2\\\\hat\\\\beta_2 x $$<span class=\"step\">Step 3: Compute its variance.</span>Using $\\\\mathrm{var}(aA + bB) = a^2\\\\mathrm{var}(A) + b^2\\\\mathrm{var}(B) + 2ab\\\\,\\\\mathrm{cov}(A,B)$ with $a = 1$, $b = 2x$:$$ \\\\mathrm{var}(\\\\hat\\\\beta_1 + 2\\\\hat\\\\beta_2 x) = \\\\mathrm{var}(\\\\hat\\\\beta_1) + 4x^2\\\\,\\\\mathrm{var}(\\\\hat\\\\beta_2) + 4x\\\\,\\\\mathrm{cov}(\\\\hat\\\\beta_1, \\\\hat\\\\beta_2) $$<div class=\"note\">The covariance term is essential — ignoring it generally gives wrong standard errors.</div><span class=\"step\">Step 4: Form the $t$-statistic for $H_0: \\\\beta_1 + 2\\\\beta_2 x = b$.</span>$$ t = \\\\frac{\\\\hat\\\\beta_1 + 2\\\\hat\\\\beta_2 x - b}{\\\\mathrm{se}(\\\\hat\\\\beta_1 + 2\\\\hat\\\\beta_2 x)} $$<span class=\"step\">Step 5: Form the CI.</span>$$ \\\\big(\\\\hat\\\\beta_1 + 2\\\\hat\\\\beta_2 x\\\\big) \\\\pm c_\\\\alpha\\\\,\\\\mathrm{se}(\\\\hat\\\\beta_1 + 2\\\\hat\\\\beta_2 x) $$<div class=\"conclude\">$$ \\\\boxed{\\\\widehat{ME}(x) = \\\\hat\\\\beta_1 + 2\\\\hat\\\\beta_2 x,\\\\quad \\\\mathrm{var} = \\\\mathrm{var}(\\\\hat\\\\beta_1) + 4x^2\\\\mathrm{var}(\\\\hat\\\\beta_2) + 4x\\\\,\\\\mathrm{cov}(\\\\hat\\\\beta_1, \\\\hat\\\\beta_2)} $$</div>",

  "2.12": "<span class=\"step\">Step 1: Decompose the deviation of $Y_i$ from $\\\\overline Y$.</span>Add and subtract $\\\\hat Y_i$:$$ Y_i - \\\\overline Y = (Y_i - \\\\hat Y_i) + (\\\\hat Y_i - \\\\overline Y) = \\\\hat u_i + (\\\\hat Y_i - \\\\overline Y) $$<span class=\"step\">Step 2: Square both sides and sum over $i$.</span>Apply $(a+b)^2 = a^2 + b^2 + 2ab$:$$ \\\\sum_{i=1}^n (Y_i - \\\\overline Y)^2 = \\\\sum_{i=1}^n \\\\hat u_i^2 + \\\\sum_{i=1}^n (\\\\hat Y_i - \\\\overline Y)^2 + 2\\\\sum_{i=1}^n \\\\hat u_i(\\\\hat Y_i - \\\\overline Y) $$<span class=\"step\">Step 3: Show the cross term vanishes.</span>Expand:$$ \\\\sum_i \\\\hat u_i(\\\\hat Y_i - \\\\overline Y) = \\\\sum_i \\\\hat u_i \\\\hat Y_i - \\\\overline Y \\\\sum_i \\\\hat u_i $$By <span class=\"assume\">OLS normal equations</span> from 1.6: $\\\\sum_i \\\\hat u_i = 0$ and $\\\\sum_i X_{l,i}\\\\hat u_i = 0$ for each regressor $l$. Since $\\\\hat Y_i = \\\\hat\\\\beta_0 + \\\\sum_l \\\\hat\\\\beta_l X_{l,i}$ is a linear combination of the regressors:$$ \\\\sum_i \\\\hat u_i \\\\hat Y_i = \\\\hat\\\\beta_0 \\\\sum_i \\\\hat u_i + \\\\sum_l \\\\hat\\\\beta_l \\\\sum_i X_{l,i}\\\\hat u_i = 0 + 0 = 0 $$And $\\\\overline Y \\\\sum_i \\\\hat u_i = \\\\overline Y \\\\cdot 0 = 0$. Therefore the cross term is zero.<span class=\"step\">Step 4: Identify the three sums of squares.</span>$$ TSS = \\\\sum_i (Y_i - \\\\overline Y)^2,\\\\quad SSR = \\\\sum_i \\\\hat u_i^2,\\\\quad ESS = \\\\sum_i (\\\\hat Y_i - \\\\overline Y)^2 $$<div class=\"conclude\">$$ \\\\boxed{TSS = SSR + ESS,\\\\qquad R^2 = \\\\dfrac{ESS}{TSS} = 1 - \\\\dfrac{SSR}{TSS}} $$</div>",

  "2.14": "<span class=\"step\">Step 1: Recall the simple-regression identity.</span>For one regressor:$$ R^2 = \\\\hat{\\\\mathrm{corr}}(X, Y)^2 $$<span class=\"step\">Step 2: Recall affine invariance of correlation.</span>For any constants $a, b, c, d$ with $b, d \\\\ne 0$:$$ \\\\hat{\\\\mathrm{corr}}(a + bX,\\\\,c + dY) = \\\\mathrm{sign}(bd)\\\\cdot\\\\hat{\\\\mathrm{corr}}(X, Y) $$Squaring removes the sign:$$ \\\\hat{\\\\mathrm{corr}}(a + bX,\\\\,c + dY)^2 = \\\\hat{\\\\mathrm{corr}}(X, Y)^2 $$<span class=\"step\">Step 3: Conclude for simple regression.</span>$R^2$ is invariant to affine rescalings of $X$ and $Y$.<span class=\"step\">Step 4: Multivariate case via FWL.</span>By <span class=\"assume\">FWL</span>, the partial $R^2$ for regressor $X_l$ is the squared correlation between residualised $Y$ and residualised $X_l$. Affine rescalings of any single regressor leave both residualisations invariant, so $R^2$ is unchanged.<div class=\"conclude\">$$ \\\\boxed{R^2 \\\\text{ is invariant to affine rescaling of regressors and the response.}} $$</div>",

  "3.1": "<span class=\"step\">Step 1: Recall the OLS probability limit.</span>By 1.7:$$ \\\\hat\\\\beta_1 \\\\xrightarrow{p} \\\\frac{\\\\mathrm{cov}(X, Y)}{\\\\mathrm{var}(X)} = \\\\rho_1 $$<span class=\"step\">Step 2: Substitute the structural equation $Y = \\\\beta_0 + \\\\beta_1 X + u$.</span>$$ \\\\mathrm{cov}(X, Y) = \\\\mathrm{cov}(X,\\\\,\\\\beta_0 + \\\\beta_1 X + u) = \\\\beta_1\\\\,\\\\mathrm{var}(X) + \\\\mathrm{cov}(X, u) $$Divide:$$ \\\\rho_1 = \\\\frac{\\\\mathrm{cov}(X, Y)}{\\\\mathrm{var}(X)} = \\\\beta_1 + \\\\frac{\\\\mathrm{cov}(X, u)}{\\\\mathrm{var}(X)} $$<span class=\"step\">Step 3: When OR fails.</span>If $\\\\mathrm{cov}(X, u) \\\\ne 0$ (so <span class=\"assume\">OR is violated</span>):$$ \\\\hat\\\\beta_1 \\\\xrightarrow{p} \\\\beta_1 + \\\\frac{\\\\mathrm{cov}(X, u)}{\\\\mathrm{var}(X)} \\\\ne \\\\beta_1 $$<div class=\"conclude\">$$ \\\\boxed{\\\\text{Asymptotic bias} = \\\\dfrac{\\\\mathrm{cov}(X, u)}{\\\\mathrm{var}(X)}} $$</div><div class=\"note\">The bias does not vanish as $n \\\\to \\\\infty$. More data does not cure endogeneity.</div>",

  "3.2": "<span class=\"step\">Step 1: Write the CEF for binary $D \\\\in \\\\{0, 1\\\\}$.</span>An indicator function trick: for $D \\\\in \\\\{0, 1\\\\}$,$$ \\\\mathbb{E}[Y\\\\mid D] = \\\\mathbb{E}[Y\\\\mid D = 0]\\\\cdot(1 - D) + \\\\mathbb{E}[Y\\\\mid D = 1]\\\\cdot D $$<span class=\"step\">Step 2: Rewrite as an affine function of $D$.</span>$$ \\\\mathbb{E}[Y\\\\mid D] = \\\\mathbb{E}[Y\\\\mid D = 0] + \\\\big(\\\\mathbb{E}[Y\\\\mid D = 1] - \\\\mathbb{E}[Y\\\\mid D = 0]\\\\big)D $$The CEF is exactly linear in $D$.<span class=\"step\">Step 3: Match to the linear regression form.</span>By 1.5 (CEF coincides with population linear regression when CEF is linear):$$ \\\\rho_0 = \\\\mathbb{E}[Y\\\\mid D = 0] $$$$ \\\\rho_1 = \\\\mathbb{E}[Y\\\\mid D = 1] - \\\\mathbb{E}[Y\\\\mid D = 0] $$<span class=\"step\">Step 4: Tie to the causal slope under random assignment.</span>Under <span class=\"assume\">random assignment</span>, $D$ is independent of potential outcomes, hence $\\\\mathbb{E}[u\\\\mid D] = 0$ (MI), so OR holds and by 1.4 $\\\\beta_1 = \\\\rho_1$.<div class=\"conclude\">$$ \\\\boxed{\\\\beta_1 = \\\\mathbb{E}[Y\\\\mid D = 1] - \\\\mathbb{E}[Y\\\\mid D = 0] = \\\\text{ATE}} $$</div>",

  "3.5": "<span class=\"step\">Step 1: Variance of the difference-in-means estimator.</span>For the simple two-arm trial with $n_T$ treated and $n_C$ control:$$ \\\\mathrm{var}(\\\\hat\\\\tau) = \\\\sigma^2\\\\left(\\\\frac{1}{n_T} + \\\\frac{1}{n_C}\\\\right) $$under <span class=\"assume\">homoskedasticity: $\\\\sigma_T^2 = \\\\sigma_C^2 = \\\\sigma^2$</span>.<span class=\"step\">Step 2: Impose the budget constraint $n_T + n_C = N$.</span>Substitute $n_C = N - n_T$:$$ f(n_T) = \\\\sigma^2\\\\left[\\\\frac{1}{n_T} + \\\\frac{1}{N - n_T}\\\\right] $$<span class=\"step\">Step 3: First-order condition.</span>$$ f'(n_T) = \\\\sigma^2\\\\left[-\\\\frac{1}{n_T^2} + \\\\frac{1}{(N - n_T)^2}\\\\right] = 0 $$<span class=\"step\">Step 4: Solve.</span>$$ \\\\frac{1}{n_T^2} = \\\\frac{1}{(N - n_T)^2} \\\\;\\\\Rightarrow\\\\; (N - n_T)^2 = n_T^2 \\\\;\\\\Rightarrow\\\\; n_T = \\\\frac{N}{2} $$<div class=\"conclude\">$$ \\\\boxed{n_T^\\\\star = n_C^\\\\star = N/2} $$</div><span class=\"step\">Step 5: Unequal-variance case (Neyman allocation).</span>Drop homoskedasticity. Variance becomes $\\\\sigma_1^2/n_T + \\\\sigma_0^2/n_C$. The Lagrangian for minimising under $n_T + n_C = N$ yields:$$ \\\\frac{\\\\sigma_1^2}{n_T^2} = \\\\frac{\\\\sigma_0^2}{n_C^2} \\\\;\\\\Rightarrow\\\\; \\\\frac{n_T}{n_C} = \\\\frac{\\\\sigma_1}{\\\\sigma_0} $$<div class=\"note\">Allocate proportionally to the within-group standard deviation: bigger arm where outcomes are noisier.</div>",

  "3.6": "<span class=\"substep\">Part A: Why we must control for stratifiers.</span><span class=\"step\">Step 1: Without controls, OR is broken.</span>If both $X$ (treatment) and $Y$ depend on a stratifier $W$, then $\\\\mathrm{cov}(X, u) \\\\ne 0$ where $u$ contains the $W$-dependence. By 3.1 the simple regression is inconsistent.<span class=\"step\">Step 2: Recover $\\\\beta_1$ by including $W$.</span>Regress $Y$ on $(X, W)$. By <span class=\"assume\">FWL</span> (1.9):$$ \\\\hat\\\\beta_1 \\\\xrightarrow{p} \\\\frac{\\\\mathrm{cov}(Y, \\\\tilde X)}{\\\\mathrm{var}(\\\\tilde X)} $$where $\\\\tilde X$ is the residual from regressing $X$ on $W$. By the FWL algebra in 1.9, this equals $\\\\beta_1$.<span class=\"substep\">Part B: Precision gain from including $W$.</span><span class=\"step\">Step 1: Recall the variance formula from 2.2.</span>$$ \\\\mathrm{var}(\\\\hat\\\\beta_1) \\\\propto \\\\frac{\\\\mathrm{var}(u)}{\\\\mathrm{var}(\\\\tilde X_1)} $$<span class=\"step\">Step 2: Effect of including a $Y$-relevant control.</span>Including $W$ correlated with $Y$ (but uncorrelated with treatment in a randomised trial) shrinks $\\\\mathrm{var}(u)$ noticeably without much shrinking $\\\\mathrm{var}(\\\\tilde X_1)$ (since $\\\\mathrm{cov}(X, W) \\\\approx 0$).<div class=\"conclude\">$$ \\\\boxed{\\\\text{Including pre-treatment controls } W \\\\text{ correlated with } Y \\\\text{ lowers } \\\\mathrm{se}(\\\\hat\\\\beta_1).} $$</div>",

  "3.7": "<span class=\"step\">Step 1: State the structural model and the measurement error.</span>True model:$$ Y = \\\\beta_0 + \\\\beta_1 X + u,\\\\quad \\\\text{<span class=\\\"assume\\\">OR: $\\\\mathrm{cov}(X, u) = 0$</span>} $$Observed outcome:$$ Y^* = Y + e_y $$with <span class=\"assume\">classical measurement error: $\\\\mathbb{E}e_y = 0,\\\\;\\\\mathrm{cov}(X, e_y) = 0,\\\\;\\\\mathrm{cov}(u, e_y) = 0$</span>.<span class=\"step\">Step 2: Substitute into the estimable model.</span>$$ Y^* = Y + e_y = \\\\beta_0 + \\\\beta_1 X + (u + e_y) =: \\\\beta_0 + \\\\beta_1 X + \\\\eta $$<span class=\"step\">Step 3: Check OR for the new error $\\\\eta = u + e_y$.</span>$$ \\\\mathrm{cov}(X, \\\\eta) = \\\\mathrm{cov}(X, u) + \\\\mathrm{cov}(X, e_y) = 0 + 0 = 0 $$So OR holds in the estimable model and OLS is consistent for $\\\\beta_1$.<span class=\"step\">Step 4: Variance impact.</span>$$ \\\\mathrm{var}(\\\\eta) = \\\\mathrm{var}(u) + \\\\mathrm{var}(e_y) > \\\\mathrm{var}(u) $$So standard errors inflate, but estimates remain unbiased and consistent.<div class=\"conclude\">$$ \\\\boxed{\\\\text{Classical ME in } Y \\\\text{: OLS still consistent; SEs larger.}} $$</div>",

  "3.8": "<span class=\"step\">Step 1: State the model and the measurement error in $X$.</span>True model:$$ Y = \\\\beta_0 + \\\\beta_1 X + u,\\\\quad \\\\mathrm{cov}(X, u) = 0 $$Observed regressor:$$ X^* = X + e_x $$with <span class=\"assume\">classical ME: $e_x \\\\perp (X, u)$, $\\\\mathbb{E}e_x = 0$</span>.<span class=\"step\">Step 2: Substitute $X = X^* - e_x$ into the structural equation.</span>$$ Y = \\\\beta_0 + \\\\beta_1(X^* - e_x) + u = \\\\beta_0 + \\\\beta_1 X^* + (u - \\\\beta_1 e_x) =: \\\\beta_0 + \\\\beta_1 X^* + \\\\varepsilon $$<span class=\"step\">Step 3: Check OR for the new error $\\\\varepsilon = u - \\\\beta_1 e_x$.</span>$$ \\\\mathrm{cov}(X^*, \\\\varepsilon) = \\\\mathrm{cov}(X + e_x,\\\\,u - \\\\beta_1 e_x) $$Expanding using independence (so $\\\\mathrm{cov}(X, e_x) = 0$, $\\\\mathrm{cov}(X, u) = 0$ by OR, $\\\\mathrm{cov}(e_x, u) = 0$):$$ = -\\\\beta_1\\\\,\\\\mathrm{cov}(X, e_x) + \\\\mathrm{cov}(e_x, u) - \\\\beta_1\\\\,\\\\mathrm{var}(e_x) + \\\\mathrm{cov}(X, u) = -\\\\beta_1\\\\,\\\\mathrm{var}(e_x) \\\\ne 0 $$OR is violated for the estimable regression.<span class=\"step\">Step 4: Compute the population regression slope.</span>$$ \\\\beta_1^* = \\\\frac{\\\\mathrm{cov}(Y, X^*)}{\\\\mathrm{var}(X^*)} $$Numerator:$$ \\\\mathrm{cov}(Y, X^*) = \\\\mathrm{cov}(\\\\beta_0 + \\\\beta_1 X + u,\\\\,X + e_x) = \\\\beta_1\\\\,\\\\mathrm{var}(X) $$(other terms zero by independence and OR).Denominator:$$ \\\\mathrm{var}(X^*) = \\\\mathrm{var}(X + e_x) = \\\\mathrm{var}(X) + \\\\mathrm{var}(e_x) $$<span class=\"step\">Step 5: Form the ratio.</span>$$ \\\\beta_1^* = \\\\beta_1\\\\cdot\\\\frac{\\\\mathrm{var}(X)}{\\\\mathrm{var}(X) + \\\\mathrm{var}(e_x)} $$The factor lies in $[0, 1]$, biasing $\\\\beta_1^*$ toward zero.<div class=\"conclude\">$$ \\\\boxed{\\\\beta_1^* = \\\\beta_1\\\\,\\\\dfrac{\\\\mathrm{var}(X)}{\\\\mathrm{var}(X) + \\\\mathrm{var}(e_x)} \\\\;\\\\;\\\\text{(attenuation bias)}} $$</div>",

  "3.9": "<span class=\"step\">Step 1: State the simultaneous demand-supply system.</span>$$ Q_d = \\\\beta_0 + \\\\beta_1 P + \\\\beta_2 X + u\\\\quad\\\\text{(demand)} $$$$ Q_s = \\\\gamma_0 + \\\\gamma_1 P + v\\\\quad\\\\text{(supply)} $$Market clearing:$$ Q_d = Q_s = Q $$<span class=\"step\">Step 2: Equate the two and solve for $P$.</span>$$ \\\\beta_0 + \\\\beta_1 P + \\\\beta_2 X + u = \\\\gamma_0 + \\\\gamma_1 P + v $$Move all $P$ terms to one side:$$ (\\\\gamma_1 - \\\\beta_1) P = (\\\\beta_0 - \\\\gamma_0) + \\\\beta_2 X + (u - v) $$<span class=\"assume\">Assume $\\\\gamma_1 \\\\ne \\\\beta_1$</span> (slopes differ; the system is identified). Divide:$$ P = \\\\frac{\\\\beta_0 - \\\\gamma_0}{\\\\gamma_1 - \\\\beta_1} + \\\\frac{\\\\beta_2}{\\\\gamma_1 - \\\\beta_1}X + \\\\frac{u - v}{\\\\gamma_1 - \\\\beta_1} $$<span class=\"step\">Step 3: Compute $\\\\mathrm{cov}(P, u)$.</span>The only $u$-dependent piece of $P$ is the last term. Assuming <span class=\"assume\">$\\\\mathbb{E}[u\\\\mid v] = 0$ and $\\\\mathrm{cov}(X, u) = 0$</span>:$$ \\\\mathrm{cov}(P, u) = \\\\frac{1}{\\\\gamma_1 - \\\\beta_1}\\\\,\\\\mathrm{cov}(u - v, u) = \\\\frac{\\\\mathrm{var}(u)}{\\\\gamma_1 - \\\\beta_1} \\\\ne 0 $$<span class=\"step\">Step 4: Conclude.</span>$P$ is endogenous in the demand equation. By 3.1, OLS of $Q$ on $P$ does not consistently estimate $\\\\beta_1$.<div class=\"conclude\">$$ \\\\boxed{\\\\mathrm{cov}(P, u) = \\\\dfrac{\\\\mathrm{var}(u)}{\\\\gamma_1 - \\\\beta_1} \\\\ne 0 \\\\;\\\\Rightarrow\\\\; \\\\text{simultaneity bias}} $$</div>",

  "3.11": "<span class=\"step\">Step 1: State the structural model and instrument conditions.</span>$$ Y = \\\\beta_0 + \\\\beta_1 X + u $$with <span class=\"assume\">Z1 (instrument exogeneity): $\\\\mathrm{cov}(Z, u) = 0$</span> and <span class=\"assume\">Z2 (instrument relevance): $\\\\mathrm{cov}(X, Z) \\\\ne 0$</span>.<span class=\"step\">Step 2: First stage.</span>Project $X$ on $Z$:$$ X = \\\\pi_0 + \\\\pi_1 Z + v,\\\\quad \\\\pi_1 = \\\\frac{\\\\mathrm{cov}(X, Z)}{\\\\mathrm{var}(Z)} \\\\ne 0 $$<span class=\"step\">Step 3: Substitute the first stage into the structural equation (the reduced form).</span>$$ Y = \\\\beta_0 + \\\\beta_1(\\\\pi_0 + \\\\pi_1 Z + v) + u = (\\\\beta_0 + \\\\beta_1\\\\pi_0) + \\\\beta_1\\\\pi_1 Z + (\\\\beta_1 v + u) $$Define:$$ \\\\gamma_0 = \\\\beta_0 + \\\\beta_1\\\\pi_0,\\\\quad \\\\gamma_1 = \\\\beta_1\\\\pi_1,\\\\quad \\\\varepsilon = \\\\beta_1 v + u $$So:$$ Y = \\\\gamma_0 + \\\\gamma_1 Z + \\\\varepsilon $$<span class=\"step\">Step 4: Check OR for the reduced form.</span>$$ \\\\mathrm{cov}(Z, \\\\varepsilon) = \\\\beta_1\\\\,\\\\mathrm{cov}(Z, v) + \\\\mathrm{cov}(Z, u) $$Both are zero: the first because $v$ is the first-stage residual orthogonal to $Z$ by construction, the second by Z1. So OR holds for the reduced form.<span class=\"step\">Step 5: OLS in reduced form and first stage are consistent.</span>$$ \\\\hat\\\\gamma_1 \\\\xrightarrow{p} \\\\gamma_1 = \\\\beta_1\\\\pi_1,\\\\qquad \\\\hat\\\\pi_1 \\\\xrightarrow{p} \\\\pi_1 $$<span class=\"step\">Step 6: Indirect Least Squares (Continuous Mapping).</span>$$ \\\\hat\\\\beta_1^{ILS} := \\\\frac{\\\\hat\\\\gamma_1}{\\\\hat\\\\pi_1} \\\\xrightarrow{p} \\\\frac{\\\\beta_1\\\\pi_1}{\\\\pi_1} = \\\\beta_1 $$<span class=\"step\">Step 7: Express in covariances.</span>Using $\\\\gamma_1 = \\\\mathrm{cov}(Y, Z)/\\\\mathrm{var}(Z)$ and $\\\\pi_1 = \\\\mathrm{cov}(X, Z)/\\\\mathrm{var}(Z)$:$$ \\\\hat\\\\beta_1^{ILS} \\\\xrightarrow{p} \\\\frac{\\\\mathrm{cov}(Y, Z)}{\\\\mathrm{cov}(X, Z)} $$<div class=\"conclude\">$$ \\\\boxed{\\\\hat\\\\beta_1^{ILS} \\\\xrightarrow{p} \\\\beta_1 = \\\\dfrac{\\\\mathrm{cov}(Y, Z)}{\\\\mathrm{cov}(X, Z)}} $$</div>",

  "3.12": "<span class=\"step\">Step 1: Define the first-stage projection.</span>$$ X^* := \\\\pi_0 + \\\\pi_1 Z $$$$ v := X - X^* $$<span class=\"step\">Step 2: Substitute $X = X^* + v$ into the structural equation.</span>$$ Y = \\\\beta_0 + \\\\beta_1(X^* + v) + u = \\\\beta_0 + \\\\beta_1 X^* + (u + \\\\beta_1 v) =: \\\\beta_0 + \\\\beta_1 X^* + \\\\varepsilon $$<span class=\"step\">Step 3: Check OR for the regression of $Y$ on $X^*$.</span>$$ \\\\mathrm{cov}(X^*, \\\\varepsilon) = \\\\beta_1\\\\,\\\\mathrm{cov}(X^*, v) + \\\\mathrm{cov}(X^*, u) $$The first term is zero because $X^*$ is a function of $Z$ only and $v = X - X^*$ is by construction the first-stage residual orthogonal to $Z$, hence to any function of $Z$. The second term is zero by <span class=\"assume\">Z1 (instrument exogeneity)</span>: $X^*$ depends only on $Z$, and $\\\\mathrm{cov}(Z, u) = 0$.$$ \\\\mathrm{cov}(X^*, \\\\varepsilon) = 0 $$<span class=\"step\">Step 4: OLS of $Y$ on $X^*$ is consistent for $\\\\beta_1$.</span>OR holds for this projected regression. In practice we use $\\\\hat X^* = \\\\hat\\\\pi_0 + \\\\hat\\\\pi_1 Z$, and $\\\\hat\\\\pi_j \\\\xrightarrow{p} \\\\pi_j$, so 2SLS converges in probability to $\\\\beta_1$:$$ \\\\hat\\\\beta_1^{2SLS} \\\\xrightarrow{p} \\\\beta_1 $$<span class=\"step\">Step 5: Generalisation to multiple instruments.</span>With $m \\\\ge 1$ instruments, $X^* = \\\\pi_0 + \\\\sum_j \\\\pi_j Z_j$ and the same logic applies: any function of the $Z_j$'s is uncorrelated with $u$ by Z1.<div class=\"conclude\">$$ \\\\boxed{\\\\hat\\\\beta_1^{2SLS} \\\\xrightarrow{p} \\\\beta_1 \\\\;\\\\;\\\\text{under Z1 and Z2}} $$</div>",

  "3.14": "<span class=\"step\">Step 1: Set up the 2SLS estimator's asymptotic distribution.</span>By an argument analogous to 1.7, with $X$ replaced by $X^*$ (the population projection of $X$ on the instruments):$$ \\\\sqrt n(\\\\hat\\\\beta_1^{2SLS} - \\\\beta_1) \\\\xrightarrow{d} N\\\\!\\\\left(0,\\\\;\\\\frac{\\\\mathbb{E}(X^* - \\\\mu_{X^*})^2 u^2}{[\\\\mathrm{var}(X^*)]^2}\\\\right) $$<span class=\"step\">Step 2: Specialise under homoskedasticity.</span>By <span class=\"assume\">homoskedasticity: $\\\\mathbb{E}[u^2\\\\mid \\\\text{instruments}] = \\\\sigma_u^2$</span>, and the same argument as in 2.2:$$ \\\\sqrt n(\\\\hat\\\\beta_1^{2SLS} - \\\\beta_1) \\\\xrightarrow{d} N\\\\!\\\\left(0,\\\\;\\\\frac{\\\\sigma_u^2}{\\\\mathrm{var}(\\\\tilde X^*)}\\\\right) $$where $\\\\tilde X^*$ is the FWL-residualised $X^*$ (in a multiple-regression setting).<span class=\"step\">Step 3: Compare to OLS variance.</span>By 2.2 the OLS variance under homoskedasticity is $\\\\sigma_u^2/\\\\mathrm{var}(\\\\tilde X)$. Since $X^* = \\\\mathbb{E}[X\\\\mid Z]$ removes any variation in $X$ not explained by $Z$:$$ \\\\mathrm{var}(\\\\tilde X^*) \\\\le \\\\mathrm{var}(\\\\tilde X) $$<div class=\"conclude\">$$ \\\\boxed{\\\\omega^2_{\\\\text{2SLS}} = \\\\dfrac{\\\\sigma_u^2}{\\\\mathrm{var}(\\\\tilde X^*)} \\\\;\\\\ge\\\\; \\\\omega^2_{\\\\text{OLS}}} $$</div><div class=\"note\">2SLS standard errors are typically larger than OLS, because the instruments use only a subset of $X$'s variation.</div>",

  "3.15": "<span class=\"step\">Step 1: Specialise the 2SLS variance to a single instrument.</span>With one instrument $Z$, $X^* = \\\\pi_0 + \\\\pi_1 Z$ so $\\\\mathrm{var}(X^*) = \\\\pi_1^2\\\\,\\\\mathrm{var}(Z)$. From 3.14 under homoskedasticity:$$ \\\\omega_{\\\\beta_1, IV}^2 = \\\\frac{\\\\sigma_u^2}{\\\\pi_1^2\\\\,\\\\mathrm{var}(Z)} $$<span class=\"step\">Step 2: Examine the limit as $\\\\pi_1 \\\\to 0$.</span>$$ \\\\pi_1 \\\\to 0 \\\\;\\\\Rightarrow\\\\; \\\\omega_{\\\\beta_1, IV}^2 \\\\to \\\\infty $$<span class=\"step\">Step 3: Implications.</span>The denominator of the limiting normal blows up. Three problems follow:<div class=\"note\"><strong>(a)</strong> Standard errors explode. <strong>(b)</strong> The asymptotic normal approximation to $\\\\hat\\\\beta_1^{2SLS}$ becomes poor (in finite samples 2SLS becomes biased toward OLS). <strong>(c)</strong> Conventional $t$-based inference fails to control size.</div><div class=\"conclude\">$$ \\\\boxed{\\\\text{Weak instrument } (\\\\pi_1 \\\\approx 0) \\\\;\\\\Rightarrow\\\\; \\\\text{normal approximation breaks down}} $$</div><div class=\"note\">Rule of thumb: first-stage F greater than 10 (Stock-Yogo) for one endogenous regressor.</div>",

  "3.16": "<span class=\"step\">Step 1: First-stage F with $m$ instruments.</span>The naive first-stage F-statistic for joint significance of all $m$ instruments has limiting distribution $F_{m,\\\\infty}$ under the null of no first-stage effect.<span class=\"step\">Step 2: Identify the relevant restriction count.</span>With $m$ instruments and one endogenous regressor identified by 2SLS, only $m - 1$ overidentifying restrictions are testable (the model would already be identified with one instrument).<span class=\"step\">Step 3: Rescale the F-statistic.</span>To match the testable degrees of freedom:$$ F^* = \\\\frac{m\\\\,F}{m - 1} $$<span class=\"step\">Step 4: Limiting distribution.</span>$$ F^* \\\\xrightarrow{d} F_{m - 1, \\\\infty} \\\\;\\\\;\\\\text{under the joint null} $$<div class=\"conclude\">$$ \\\\boxed{F^* = \\\\dfrac{mF}{m - 1} \\\\xrightarrow{d} F_{m - 1, \\\\infty}} $$</div><div class=\"note\">The Stock-Yogo critical values for weak-instrument testing assume this rescaling.</div>",

  "4.4": "<span class=\"step\">Step 1: State the AR(1) recursion.</span>$$ Y_t = \\\\beta_0 + \\\\beta_1 Y_{t-1} + u_t $$<span class=\"step\">Step 2: Substitute one period back for $Y_{t-1}$.</span>$$ Y_t = \\\\beta_0 + \\\\beta_1(\\\\beta_0 + \\\\beta_1 Y_{t-2} + u_{t-1}) + u_t = \\\\beta_0(1 + \\\\beta_1) + \\\\beta_1^2 Y_{t-2} + (u_t + \\\\beta_1 u_{t-1}) $$<span class=\"step\">Step 3: Iterate down to $Y_0$.</span>By induction:$$ Y_t = \\\\beta_0\\\\big(1 + \\\\beta_1 + \\\\beta_1^2 + \\\\dots + \\\\beta_1^{t-1}\\\\big) + \\\\beta_1^t Y_0 + \\\\sum_{s=0}^{t-1}\\\\beta_1^s\\\\,u_{t-s} $$<span class=\"step\">Step 4: Examine the three regimes.</span><span class=\"substep\">Case A: $|\\\\beta_1| < 1$ (stationary).</span>The geometric series$$ 1 + \\\\beta_1 + \\\\beta_1^2 + \\\\dots = \\\\frac{1}{1 - \\\\beta_1} $$converges. So:$$ \\\\beta_0\\\\sum_{s=0}^{t-1}\\\\beta_1^s \\\\to \\\\frac{\\\\beta_0}{1 - \\\\beta_1} =: \\\\mu $$Also $\\\\beta_1^t Y_0 \\\\to 0$, so $Y_0$'s impact dies out. Shocks $u_{t-s}$ enter with weights $\\\\beta_1^s$ that decay geometrically.<span class=\"substep\">Case B: $|\\\\beta_1| = 1$ (unit root, random walk).</span>The constant grows as $\\\\beta_0 t$ (linear trend if $\\\\beta_0 \\\\ne 0$); $Y_0$ persists; shocks accumulate with equal weights of $1$.<span class=\"substep\">Case C: $|\\\\beta_1| > 1$ (explosive).</span>$\\\\beta_1^t Y_0$ diverges; shock weights $\\\\beta_1^s$ explode; $Y_t$ leaves any bounded region almost surely.<div class=\"conclude\">$$ \\\\boxed{Y_t = \\\\beta_0\\\\sum_{s=0}^{t-1}\\\\beta_1^s + \\\\beta_1^t Y_0 + \\\\sum_{s=0}^{t-1}\\\\beta_1^s\\\\,u_{t-s}} $$</div>",

  "4.5": "<span class=\"substep\">Part A: Mean.</span><span class=\"step\">Step 1: Take expectation of the AR(1) equation.</span>From $Y_t = \\\\beta_0 + \\\\beta_1 Y_{t-1} + u_t$:$$ \\\\mathbb{E}Y_t = \\\\beta_0 + \\\\beta_1\\\\,\\\\mathbb{E}Y_{t-1} + \\\\mathbb{E}u_t $$<span class=\"step\">Step 2: Apply stationarity.</span>By <span class=\"assume\">stationarity: $\\\\mathbb{E}Y_t = \\\\mathbb{E}Y_{t-1} = \\\\mu$</span>, and <span class=\"assume\">$\\\\mathbb{E}u_t = 0$</span>:$$ \\\\mu = \\\\beta_0 + \\\\beta_1 \\\\mu $$<span class=\"step\">Step 3: Solve, assuming $\\\\beta_1 \\\\ne 1$.</span>$$ \\\\mu(1 - \\\\beta_1) = \\\\beta_0 \\\\;\\\\Rightarrow\\\\; \\\\mu = \\\\frac{\\\\beta_0}{1 - \\\\beta_1} $$<div class=\"conclude\">$$ \\\\boxed{\\\\mu = \\\\dfrac{\\\\beta_0}{1 - \\\\beta_1}} $$</div><span class=\"substep\">Part B: Variance.</span><span class=\"step\">Step 1: Take variance of both sides.</span>$$ \\\\mathrm{var}(Y_t) = \\\\beta_1^2\\\\,\\\\mathrm{var}(Y_{t-1}) + \\\\mathrm{var}(u_t) + 2\\\\beta_1\\\\,\\\\mathrm{cov}(Y_{t-1}, u_t) $$<span class=\"step\">Step 2: Kill the cross term.</span>By the <span class=\"assume\">martingale-difference property: $\\\\mathbb{E}[u_t\\\\mid \\\\mathcal{Y}_{t-1}] = 0$</span>, so $\\\\mathrm{cov}(Y_{t-1}, u_t) = 0$:$$ \\\\mathrm{var}(Y_t) = \\\\beta_1^2\\\\,\\\\mathrm{var}(Y_{t-1}) + \\\\sigma_u^2 $$<span class=\"step\">Step 3: Apply stationarity ($\\\\mathrm{var}(Y_t) = \\\\mathrm{var}(Y_{t-1}) = \\\\sigma_Y^2$).</span>$$ \\\\sigma_Y^2 = \\\\beta_1^2 \\\\sigma_Y^2 + \\\\sigma_u^2 \\\\;\\\\Rightarrow\\\\; \\\\sigma_Y^2(1 - \\\\beta_1^2) = \\\\sigma_u^2 $$<span class=\"step\">Step 4: Solve, assuming <span class=\"assume\">$|\\\\beta_1| < 1$</span>.</span>$$ \\\\sigma_Y^2 = \\\\frac{\\\\sigma_u^2}{1 - \\\\beta_1^2} $$<div class=\"conclude\">$$ \\\\boxed{\\\\sigma_Y^2 = \\\\dfrac{\\\\sigma_u^2}{1 - \\\\beta_1^2}} $$</div><span class=\"substep\">Part C: Autocovariance at lag $h$.</span><span class=\"step\">Step 1: Take covariance of $Y_t$ with $Y_{t-h}$.</span>$$ \\\\mathrm{cov}(Y_t, Y_{t-h}) = \\\\beta_1\\\\,\\\\mathrm{cov}(Y_{t-1}, Y_{t-h}) + \\\\mathrm{cov}(u_t, Y_{t-h}) $$The second term is zero because $u_t$ is a martingale-difference innovation, so it is uncorrelated with $Y_{t-h}$ for $h \\\\ge 1$.<span class=\"step\">Step 2: Iterate $h$ times.</span>$$ \\\\mathrm{cov}(Y_t, Y_{t-h}) = \\\\beta_1 \\\\cdot \\\\beta_1 \\\\cdots \\\\beta_1\\\\,\\\\mathrm{cov}(Y_{t-h}, Y_{t-h}) = \\\\beta_1^h\\\\,\\\\sigma_Y^2 $$<div class=\"conclude\">$$ \\\\boxed{\\\\gamma_h = \\\\beta_1^h\\\\,\\\\sigma_Y^2} $$</div>",

  "4.6": "<span class=\"step\">Step 1: State the assumption.</span>Innovations are a martingale-difference sequence with respect to the past:$$ \\\\mathbb{E}[u_t\\\\mid \\\\mathcal{Y}_{t-1}] = 0 $$where $\\\\mathcal{Y}_{t-1} = (Y_{t-1}, Y_{t-2}, \\\\dots)$.<span class=\"step\">Step 2: Verify $\\\\mathbb{E}u_t = 0$.</span>By <span class=\"assume\">LIE</span>:$$ \\\\mathbb{E}u_t = \\\\mathbb{E}\\\\big[\\\\mathbb{E}[u_t\\\\mid \\\\mathcal{Y}_{t-1}]\\\\big] = \\\\mathbb{E}[0] = 0 $$<span class=\"step\">Step 3: Verify $\\\\mathbb{E}[Y_{t-i}u_t] = 0$ for $i \\\\ge 1$.</span>Since $Y_{t-i}\\\\in\\\\mathcal{Y}_{t-1}$, by the <span class=\"assume\">conditioning rule</span>:$$ \\\\mathbb{E}[Y_{t-i}u_t] = \\\\mathbb{E}\\\\big[\\\\mathbb{E}[Y_{t-i}u_t\\\\mid \\\\mathcal{Y}_{t-1}]\\\\big] = \\\\mathbb{E}\\\\big[Y_{t-i}\\\\,\\\\mathbb{E}[u_t\\\\mid \\\\mathcal{Y}_{t-1}]\\\\big] = \\\\mathbb{E}[Y_{t-i}\\\\cdot 0] = 0 $$<span class=\"step\">Step 4: Conclude OR for the AR(p) regression.</span>OR holds with respect to all $p$ lagged regressors. Population AR(p) coefficients equal structural $\\\\beta_i$.<span class=\"step\">Step 5: Consistency of $\\\\hat\\\\beta_i$.</span>By the <span class=\"assume\">LLN for stationary ergodic processes</span> (replacing the iid LLN), sample averages converge to population expectations. The OLS algebra of 1.7 carries over with this LLN, giving:$$ \\\\hat\\\\beta_i \\\\xrightarrow{p} \\\\beta_i $$<div class=\"conclude\">$$ \\\\boxed{\\\\text{MD innovations} + \\\\text{stationarity} \\\\;\\\\Rightarrow\\\\; \\\\hat\\\\beta_i \\\\xrightarrow{p} \\\\beta_i} $$</div>",

  "4.7": "<span class=\"substep\">Part A: Optimal forecast equals the CEF.</span><div class=\"note\">By 0.8 (Part C), the function of past data $\\\\mathcal{Y}_T$ that minimises $\\\\mathbb{E}[Y_{T+1} - m(\\\\mathcal{Y}_T)]^2$ is the conditional expectation:$$ Y_{T+1\\\\mid T} := \\\\mathbb{E}[Y_{T+1}\\\\mid \\\\mathcal{Y}_T] $$</div><span class=\"substep\">Part B: $h$-step AR(1) forecast.</span><span class=\"step\">Step 1: Apply the chain rule of conditioning.</span>For an AR(1) under MD innovations:$$ Y_{T+h\\\\mid T} = \\\\mathbb{E}[\\\\beta_0 + \\\\beta_1 Y_{T+h-1} + u_{T+h}\\\\mid \\\\mathcal{Y}_T] = \\\\beta_0 + \\\\beta_1\\\\,Y_{T+h-1\\\\mid T} $$<span class=\"step\">Step 2: Centre around the unconditional mean $\\\\mu = \\\\beta_0/(1 - \\\\beta_1)$.</span>Subtract $\\\\mu$ from both sides and use $\\\\mu = \\\\beta_0 + \\\\beta_1\\\\mu$:$$ Y_{T+h\\\\mid T} - \\\\mu = \\\\beta_1(Y_{T+h-1\\\\mid T} - \\\\mu) $$<span class=\"step\">Step 3: Iterate down to $h = 0$, with $Y_{T\\\\mid T} = Y_T$.</span>$$ Y_{T+h\\\\mid T} - \\\\mu = \\\\beta_1^h(Y_T - \\\\mu) $$<div class=\"conclude\">$$ \\\\boxed{Y_{T+h\\\\mid T} = \\\\mu + \\\\beta_1^h(Y_T - \\\\mu)} $$</div><span class=\"step\">Step 4: Long-horizon limit.</span>Under <span class=\"assume\">$|\\\\beta_1| < 1$</span>, $\\\\beta_1^h \\\\to 0$, so $Y_{T+h\\\\mid T} \\\\to \\\\mu$.<span class=\"substep\">Part C: MSFE decomposition.</span><span class=\"step\">Step 1: Decompose the one-step forecast error.</span>Using the estimated forecast $\\\\hat Y_{T+1\\\\mid T}$ and the optimal (unknown) one $Y_{T+1\\\\mid T}$:$$ \\\\hat e_{T+1\\\\mid T} = Y_{T+1} - \\\\hat Y_{T+1\\\\mid T} = \\\\underbrace{(Y_{T+1} - Y_{T+1\\\\mid T})}_{= u_{T+1}} + \\\\underbrace{(Y_{T+1\\\\mid T} - \\\\hat Y_{T+1\\\\mid T})}_{\\\\text{parameter estimation error}} $$<span class=\"step\">Step 2: Show the two parts are uncorrelated.</span>$u_{T+1}$ is independent of $\\\\mathcal{Y}_T$ (by MD), and the parameter estimation error depends only on $\\\\mathcal{Y}_T$. So their covariance is zero.<span class=\"step\">Step 3: Take the squared expectation.</span>$$ \\\\mathrm{MSFE} = \\\\mathbb{E}\\\\hat e_{T+1\\\\mid T}^2 = \\\\mathrm{var}(u) + \\\\mathbb{E}(\\\\hat Y_{T+1\\\\mid T} - Y_{T+1\\\\mid T})^2 $$<div class=\"conclude\">$$ \\\\boxed{\\\\mathrm{MSFE} = \\\\underbrace{\\\\sigma_u^2}_{\\\\text{irreducible}} + \\\\underbrace{\\\\mathbb{E}(\\\\hat Y - Y_{\\\\mid T})^2}_{\\\\text{parameter uncertainty}}} $$</div>",

  "4.8": "<span class=\"step\">Step 1: Recall the MSFE decomposition (4.7).</span>$$ \\\\mathrm{MSFE} = \\\\sigma_u^2 + \\\\mathbb{E}(\\\\hat Y_{T+1\\\\mid T} - Y_{T+1\\\\mid T})^2 $$The second term captures parameter-estimation uncertainty.<span class=\"step\">Step 2: Why in-sample residuals understate MSFE.</span>The fitted residual $\\\\hat u_t = Y_t - \\\\hat Y_t$ is computed using OLS coefficients fitted to the same data $(Y_1, \\\\dots, Y_n)$. The fit is chosen to make $\\\\sum \\\\hat u_t^2$ small, so the in-sample residual variance underestimates the parameter-uncertainty term in 4.7:$$ \\\\hat\\\\sigma^2_{\\\\text{in-sample}} \\\\approx \\\\sigma_u^2 \\\\;\\\\;\\\\text{(misses parameter uncertainty)} $$<span class=\"step\">Step 3: Out-of-sample residuals are honest.</span>Pseudo-out-of-sample (POOS) residuals are formed using parameters fitted on data <em>before</em> period $t$. So they include both the irreducible noise and the parameter uncertainty:$$ \\\\widehat{\\\\mathrm{MSFE}}_{\\\\text{POOS}} \\\\xrightarrow{p} \\\\mathrm{MSFE} $$<div class=\"conclude\">$$ \\\\boxed{\\\\text{Use POOS, not in-sample, residuals to estimate forecast accuracy.}} $$</div>",

  "4.11": "<span class=\"step\">Step 1: State the unrestricted and restricted models.</span>Unrestricted (ADL$(p, q)$):$$ Y_t = \\\\beta_0 + \\\\sum_{i=1}^p \\\\beta_i Y_{t-i} + \\\\sum_{j=1}^q \\\\delta_j X_{t-j} + u_t $$Restricted (AR$(p)$, $H_0: \\\\delta_1 = \\\\dots = \\\\delta_q = 0$):$$ Y_t = \\\\beta_0 + \\\\sum_{i=1}^p \\\\beta_i Y_{t-i} + u_t $$<span class=\"step\">Step 2: Form the F-statistic on the $q$ restrictions.</span>$$ F = \\\\frac{(SSR_r - SSR_u)/q}{SSR_u/(T - p - q - 1)} $$<span class=\"step\">Step 3: Limiting distribution under $H_0$.</span>Under <span class=\"assume\">joint stationarity, ergodicity, finite fourth moments, and MD innovations</span>, the time-series F-test has the same limit as the cross-sectional one:$$ F \\\\xrightarrow{d} F_{q, \\\\infty} $$<span class=\"step\">Step 4: Decision.</span>Reject $H_0$ ($X$ does <em>not</em> Granger-cause $Y$) when $F > F_{q,\\\\infty}^{\\\\,1-\\\\alpha}$.<div class=\"conclude\">$$ \\\\boxed{F \\\\xrightarrow{d} F_{q, \\\\infty} \\\\;\\\\text{ under the null of no Granger-causality}} $$</div><div class=\"note\">Granger-causality is predictive content, not necessarily structural causality. It says past $X$ improves the forecast of $Y$ beyond past $Y$ alone.</div>",

  "4.14": "<span class=\"step\">Step 1: State the unit-root AR(1) with drift.</span>Set $\\\\beta_1 = 1$ in the AR(1):$$ Y_t = \\\\beta_0 + Y_{t-1} + u_t $$<span class=\"step\">Step 2: Recurse one step.</span>$$ Y_t = \\\\beta_0 + (\\\\beta_0 + Y_{t-2} + u_{t-1}) + u_t = 2\\\\beta_0 + Y_{t-2} + u_{t-1} + u_t $$<span class=\"step\">Step 3: Iterate down to $Y_0$.</span>By induction:$$ Y_t = Y_0 + \\\\beta_0\\\\,t + \\\\sum_{s=1}^t u_s $$Define the cumulative shock $U_t := \\\\sum_{s=1}^t u_s$. Then:$$ Y_t = Y_0 + \\\\beta_0\\\\,t + U_t $$<span class=\"step\">Step 4: Properties of $U_t$.</span>By <span class=\"assume\">$\\\\mathbb{E}u_s = 0$, $\\\\mathrm{var}(u_s) = \\\\sigma_u^2$, and serial uncorrelation</span>:$$ \\\\mathbb{E}U_t = 0 $$$$ \\\\mathrm{var}(U_t) = \\\\sum_{s=1}^t \\\\mathrm{var}(u_s) = t\\\\,\\\\sigma_u^2 $$<div class=\"note\">Variance grows linearly in $t$: the process spreads out over time. There is no mean reversion. With $\\\\beta_0 \\\\ne 0$ a deterministic linear trend $\\\\beta_0 t$ is also present.</div><div class=\"conclude\">$$ \\\\boxed{Y_t = Y_0 + \\\\beta_0\\\\,t + U_t,\\\\quad \\\\mathbb{E}U_t = 0,\\\\quad \\\\mathrm{var}(U_t) = t\\\\sigma_u^2} $$</div>",

  "4.18": "<span class=\"step\">Step 1: Start from the AR(1).</span>$$ Y_t = \\\\beta_0 + \\\\beta_1 Y_{t-1} + u_t $$<span class=\"step\">Step 2: Subtract $Y_{t-1}$ from both sides.</span>$$ Y_t - Y_{t-1} = \\\\beta_0 + \\\\beta_1 Y_{t-1} - Y_{t-1} + u_t $$<span class=\"step\">Step 3: Factor and rename.</span>Define $\\\\Delta Y_t := Y_t - Y_{t-1}$ and $\\\\delta := \\\\beta_1 - 1$:$$ \\\\Delta Y_t = \\\\beta_0 + (\\\\beta_1 - 1)Y_{t-1} + u_t = \\\\beta_0 + \\\\delta\\\\,Y_{t-1} + u_t $$<span class=\"step\">Step 4: Translate the unit-root hypothesis.</span>$$ H_0: \\\\beta_1 = 1 \\\\;\\\\Longleftrightarrow\\\\; H_0: \\\\delta = 0 $$$$ H_1: |\\\\beta_1| < 1 \\\\;\\\\Longleftrightarrow\\\\; H_1: \\\\delta < 0 $$<div class=\"conclude\">$$ \\\\boxed{\\\\Delta Y_t = \\\\beta_0 + \\\\delta\\\\,Y_{t-1} + u_t,\\\\quad H_0: \\\\delta = 0\\\\;\\\\text{vs.}\\\\;H_1: \\\\delta < 0} $$</div><div class=\"note\">This Dickey-Fuller transformation is what makes the unit-root test a one-sided $t$-test. Under $H_0$, however, $Y_{t-1}$ is non-stationary, so the limiting distribution of $t(\\\\delta = 0)$ is <em>not</em> normal; it follows the Dickey-Fuller distribution.</div>",

  "4.19": "<span class=\"substep\">Part A: ADF transformation (Appendix F).</span><span class=\"step\">Step 1: Start from the AR(p).</span>$$ Y_t = \\\\beta_0 + \\\\sum_{i=1}^p \\\\beta_i Y_{t-i} + u_t $$<span class=\"step\">Step 2: Define the auxiliary parameters.</span>$$ \\\\delta := \\\\sum_{i=1}^p \\\\beta_i - 1,\\\\qquad \\\\gamma_i := -\\\\sum_{j=i+1}^p \\\\beta_j \\\\;\\\\;(i = 1, \\\\dots, p - 1) $$Set $\\\\gamma_0 := \\\\delta + 1 = \\\\sum_{i=1}^p \\\\beta_i$.<span class=\"step\">Step 3: Telescoping identity.</span>By construction, $\\\\beta_i = \\\\gamma_i - \\\\gamma_{i-1}$ for $i = 1, \\\\dots, p - 1$, with $\\\\beta_p = -\\\\gamma_{p-1}$.<span class=\"step\">Step 4: Substitute and re-arrange the lag polynomial.</span>Using a summation-by-parts identity:$$ \\\\sum_{i=1}^p \\\\beta_i Y_{t-i} = -\\\\gamma_0 Y_{t-1} + \\\\sum_{i=1}^{p-1} \\\\gamma_i\\\\,\\\\Delta Y_{t-i}\\\\quad(\\\\text{wait — sign}) $$More carefully, the standard derivation gives:$$ \\\\sum_{i=1}^p \\\\beta_i Y_{t-i} = (\\\\delta + 1)Y_{t-1} + \\\\sum_{i=1}^{p-1} \\\\gamma_i\\\\,\\\\Delta Y_{t-i} $$<span class=\"step\">Step 5: Subtract $Y_{t-1}$ from both sides of the AR(p).</span>$$ \\\\Delta Y_t = \\\\beta_0 + \\\\delta\\\\,Y_{t-1} + \\\\sum_{i=1}^{p-1} \\\\gamma_i\\\\,\\\\Delta Y_{t-i} + u_t $$Set $p_\\\\Delta := p - 1$.<div class=\"conclude\">$$ \\\\boxed{\\\\Delta Y_t = \\\\beta_0 + \\\\delta\\\\,Y_{t-1} + \\\\sum_{i=1}^{p_\\\\Delta} \\\\gamma_i\\\\,\\\\Delta Y_{t-i} + u_t} $$</div><div class=\"note\">Adding $\\\\alpha\\\\,t$ on the right gives the constant-and-trend variant of the regression.</div><span class=\"substep\">Part B: The ADF test.</span><span class=\"step\">Step 1: Test the unit root via $\\\\delta$.</span>$$ H_0: \\\\delta = 0\\\\;(\\\\text{unit root}),\\\\;H_1: \\\\delta < 0\\\\;(\\\\text{stationary}) $$Use the OLS $t$-statistic on $\\\\delta$.<span class=\"step\">Step 2: Limiting distribution.</span>Under <span class=\"assume\">$H_0$ (the process is non-stationary)</span>, $Y_{t-1}$ is integrated of order $1$, so the standard CLT does not apply. The limiting distribution is the Dickey-Fuller distribution:$$ t(\\\\delta = 0) \\\\xrightarrow{d} \\\\mathrm{DF}_{cn}\\\\;(\\\\text{constant only})\\\\;\\\\text{or}\\\\;\\\\mathrm{DF}_{tr}\\\\;(\\\\text{constant + trend}) $$<div class=\"conclude\">$$ \\\\boxed{\\\\text{Reject unit root if } t(\\\\hat\\\\delta) < \\\\text{DF critical value (negative)}} $$</div>",

  "4.21": "<span class=\"step\">Step 1: Recall the Engle-Granger setup.</span>Two integrated series $Y_t, X_t$ are cointegrated if there is a $\\\\theta$ such that $\\\\xi_t := Y_t - \\\\theta X_t$ is stationary. Engle-Granger tests this in two stages.<span class=\"step\">Step 2: Stage 1 - estimate $\\\\theta$ by OLS.</span>Run the cointegrating regression:$$ Y_t = \\\\alpha + \\\\theta X_t + \\\\xi_t $$Obtain $\\\\hat\\\\theta$ and the residual:$$ \\\\hat\\\\xi_t := Y_t - \\\\hat\\\\alpha - \\\\hat\\\\theta X_t $$<span class=\"step\">Step 3: Stage 2 - run an ADF test on $\\\\hat\\\\xi_t$.</span>Apply the ADF transformation (4.19) to $\\\\hat\\\\xi_t$:$$ \\\\Delta\\\\hat\\\\xi_t = \\\\rho\\\\,\\\\hat\\\\xi_{t-1} + \\\\sum_{i=1}^{p-1} \\\\gamma_i\\\\,\\\\Delta\\\\hat\\\\xi_{t-i} + e_t $$Test:$$ H_0: \\\\rho = 0\\\\;(\\\\text{no cointegration})\\\\;\\\\text{vs.}\\\\;H_1: \\\\rho < 0\\\\;(\\\\text{cointegration}) $$<span class=\"step\">Step 4: Why we need new critical values.</span>Estimating $\\\\hat\\\\theta$ in Stage 1 uses up degrees of freedom and shifts the limiting distribution of the Stage-2 ADF $t$-statistic to the left of the standard ADF distribution. The standard DF critical values are not valid.<span class=\"step\">Step 5: Use Engle-Granger tabulated critical values.</span>The EG critical values correct for the pre-estimation of $\\\\hat\\\\theta$ and depend on the number of regressors in the cointegrating regression.<div class=\"conclude\">$$ \\\\boxed{\\\\text{Reject no cointegration if } t(\\\\hat\\\\rho) < \\\\text{Engle-Granger CV (more negative than DF CV)}} $$</div><div class=\"note\">If you reject, fit a vector error-correction model (VECM) using $\\\\hat\\\\xi_t$ as the equilibrium-error term.</div>"
};

if (typeof module !== "undefined" && module.exports) { module.exports = DERIVATIONS; }
